[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505780 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cc5eff9-4511-5ebc-9346-fef6d3ff2cd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505293} {:answer/id #uuid "61a52828-b97a-55bc-89af-abdc326b7088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505301} {:answer/id #uuid "a747a90c-e5d0-5aef-9c1b-23569a104c74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505440} {:answer/id #uuid "bf18c3df-1eff-5189-830c-ed12cf41dde6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505476} {:answer/id #uuid "cae550b1-5979-52c8-970e-b1b797158855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a747a90c-e5d0-5aef-9c1b-23569a104c74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505440} :answer-field/id #uuid "7876217b-a5c3-5d52-8219-aba955692660" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505660}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6b6577b4-d24d-5962-9f5c-436ba639df8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-73371" :question/problem "${\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)}{{sin}^{4}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\operatorname{cot}⁡2x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)}{{sin}^{4}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{4}⁡(2x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{5}⁡(2x)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{5}⁡(2x)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{u}^{-4}}{-4}){|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}(\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}){|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}(\\frac{1}{{1}^{4}}-{(\\frac{2}{1})}^{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}(1-16) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{8}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505782 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "521f1d1c-c4a9-54ce-be0a-26a5043158cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "521f1d1c-c4a9-54ce-be0a-26a5043158cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505268} :answer-field/id #uuid "3dc0e4ad-a39d-502a-8eb5-af62880a7035" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "70bc61eb-58b7-5349-9bef-f900f312799d" :question/math-academy-id "q-342615" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =2 \\\\ x & ={2}^{2} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=4.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(4),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505784 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e56c770a-5806-5999-8f0f-05f21b960997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{p}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e56c770a-5806-5999-8f0f-05f21b960997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{p}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505553} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f2227fe-2623-5005-877d-8e648618036c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7b0ffba6-735a-5d4b-89a4-021c8a5b9247" :question/math-academy-id "q-343590" :question/problem "$$\n\nGiven that $p\\ge 25,$ express $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p})$ using the definition of the inverse hyperbolic cosine.\n\n$\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p})}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\sqrt{\\frac{{p}^{4}+50{p}^{2}+625}{100{p}^{2}}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\sqrt{\\frac{{p}^{4}-50{p}^{2}+625}{100{p}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{p}^{2}-25}{10p})}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+|\\frac{{p}^{2}-25}{10p}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $p\\ge 25,$ we have $\\frac{{p}^{2}-25}{10p}>0,$ and we can remove the absolute value in the above expression. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\frac{{p}^{2}-25}{10p}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{p}{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505785 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d086c220-4627-5c91-b9c1-4b6c8a97c4be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d086c220-4627-5c91-b9c1-4b6c8a97c4be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505504} :answer-field/id #uuid "27bd1c0c-89e6-510d-8840-4d12f9be8883" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505627} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f6aaefd-82b2-597b-94e2-766881cdd3a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f6aaefd-82b2-597b-94e2-766881cdd3a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505331} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6590ac0-071c-5dc7-835a-5e92e0475c4a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505684} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e303b12d-c9eb-52a4-877a-c6da28a1a9e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e303b12d-c9eb-52a4-877a-c6da28a1a9e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505547} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1127e0f-95b4-5a48-b495-9387fefcb90d" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "821d20a5-8c94-5b58-8ee1-508076709a90" :question/math-academy-id "q-351917" :question/problem "Find the second-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{2}(x)$ of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{1-x}$ about $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n${P}_{2}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$(x-\\frac{1}{2})\\,+$\n{{field-3}}${(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ we plug $a=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(\\frac{1}{2})+{f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})(x-\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2}){(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(\\frac{1}{2}),$ ${f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}),$ and ${f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{1-x} & ⟹ &  & f(\\frac{1}{2}) & =2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{(1-x)^{2}} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =4 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2}{(1-x)^{3}} & ⟹ &  & {f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(\\frac{1}{2}),$ ${f}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2}),$ and ${f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2})$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =2+4(x-\\frac{1}{2})+16\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =2+4(x-\\frac{1}{2})+8{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505789 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ba3e473-02fb-5157-b482-3554be83ad62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505185} {:answer/id #uuid "45918914-cbad-5fa0-8b0f-566e642a966c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505246} {:answer/id #uuid "5762675e-b9b2-5628-ae14-b2f8bf8c8a64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505281} {:answer/id #uuid "68bef3d6-95b9-53bc-bfd3-a4201accc046" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505316} {:answer/id #uuid "ef341ed2-5b5f-5140-8092-d50bef9018a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505568}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ba3e473-02fb-5157-b482-3554be83ad62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505185} :answer-field/id #uuid "67fc1eae-c999-56ca-96a1-f0623bb0224c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "83ade4bd-cffc-596f-9566-1eb5e39170cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-95616" :question/problem "$$\n\n$\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{1}{4})=$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{1}{4}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+(-\\frac{1}{4})}{1-(-\\frac{1}{4})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-\\frac{1}{4}}{1+\\frac{1}{4}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\frac{3}{4}}{\\frac{5}{4}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505790 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e1275fc-45d5-56e9-921c-419284dfd186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505260} {:answer/id #uuid "64b7ad1d-e8f4-5ece-9cdb-d0b6a18c31ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+6}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505307} {:answer/id #uuid "70ac23c1-c539-56dc-ab99-2f9ae1aa9224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2+\\sqrt{6}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505335} {:answer/id #uuid "c6970587-5e60-5326-ba2f-20db18796990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{6-\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505490} {:answer/id #uuid "fbd92dee-655a-57ac-b2c7-90a821947212" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e1275fc-45d5-56e9-921c-419284dfd186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505260} :answer-field/id #uuid "7107c43c-bc1f-5f63-9a1c-c16d62962b34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "854f76b3-c5a6-5b21-803f-68a8249b452b" :question/math-academy-id "q-9601" :question/problem "$$\n\n$\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505792 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c693b7-cccd-550b-a54d-0a489e678e11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{9}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505119} {:answer/id #uuid "0c559b12-c2b6-5701-81a8-9ef77e5ea06a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505126} {:answer/id #uuid "30e5f82b-22e6-5d14-87b0-25b92fa198e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505189} {:answer/id #uuid "9a953977-58ca-5907-ba22-17c28ab4e885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505406} {:answer/id #uuid "ba6d3902-4c49-563c-8ffd-9bc430647bb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a953977-58ca-5907-ba22-17c28ab4e885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505406} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bbba225-3f98-5236-a2d6-45d639d898aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8ce8f55a-871b-590a-9437-e579e7e6b7ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-125838" :question/problem "$$\n\n$\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{9}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{9}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{5}{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505806 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d786de3f-c166-5197-8b45-69ebfef00b54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}}{1-2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d786de3f-c166-5197-8b45-69ebfef00b54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}}{1-2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505521} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7beb05c-ce8a-51e7-be2b-4e84b5896568" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b5800963-d7c6-5c24-9f66-ca5ef86b26b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-341494" :question/problem "$$\n\nGiven that $0<x<\\frac{1}{4},$ express $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(4\\sqrt{x}-1)$ using the definition of the inverse hyperbolic tangent.\n\n$\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(4\\sqrt{x}-1)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(4\\sqrt{x}-1) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+4\\sqrt{x}-1}{1-4\\sqrt{x}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4\\sqrt{x}}{2-4\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}}{1-2\\sqrt{x}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505807 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e28eb08e-4a02-5921-830b-171458c8d0d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{400}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e28eb08e-4a02-5921-830b-171458c8d0d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{400}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505545} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d270b7c-49c4-5539-9b50-53390b12221d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b66642ed-0ee4-55e3-841c-23e21fdfce5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-166818" :question/problem "Expressed as an improper fraction, the exact amount of paint available is$\\,${{field-1}}\n$\\,$liters." :question/worked-solution "The exact amount of paint is ${(\\frac{20}{9})}^{2}$ liters.\n\nWriting ${(\\frac{20}{9})}^{2}$ with multiplication signs, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{20}{9})}^{2} & =\\underbrace{\\frac{20}{9}\\times \\frac{20}{9}}_{2\\text{ times}} \\\\  & =\\frac{20\\times 20}{9\\times 9} \\\\  & =\\frac{400}{81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{20}{9})}^{2}=\\frac{400}{81}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505809 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57752369-ee0f-585d-9307-31648dc6aed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505282} {:answer/id #uuid "6cf67a66-1f7d-589b-9794-976a6ee03856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505324} {:answer/id #uuid "6e6725ad-f38e-5393-96b7-7603ac0c2f06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505329} {:answer/id #uuid "a9187687-7c45-54ed-92e1-7d05ac532cf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505442} {:answer/id #uuid "b3f34fb5-2b1a-527d-8cbd-18a361eec573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3f34fb5-2b1a-527d-8cbd-18a361eec573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505455} :answer-field/id #uuid "9163b72a-ca3f-56d5-be84-6c361f7f2037" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bcf19778-fecb-5e86-ab67-3237ac262859" :question/math-academy-id "q-73357" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡3x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡3x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡3x}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}$ and $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡3x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=3x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.$ \n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=3x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ | $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505812 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5999c02b-4e0a-5088-a1ce-199924772d19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2+w}{1-w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5999c02b-4e0a-5088-a1ce-199924772d19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2+w}{1-w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505285} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c578e06-f08e-5dc7-b0a7-5b75a8b2c043" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c284afac-baa7-5076-92a3-0d89b2214f32" :question/math-academy-id "q-341500" :question/problem "$$\n\nGiven that $-2<w<1,$ express $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{2w+1}{3})$ using the definition of the inverse hyperbolic tangent.\n\n$\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{2w+1}{3})=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{2w+1}{3}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+\\frac{2w+1}{3}}{1-\\frac{2w+1}{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\frac{3+2w+1}{3}}{\\frac{3-2w-1}{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4+2w}{2-2w}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2+w}{1-w}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505814 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2520222e-c4e3-55e1-9c0c-b0256d5a01c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-x}{1+x})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505167} {:answer/id #uuid "6325d0ab-7ae4-5d74-ad04-8971639df056" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505304} {:answer/id #uuid "6e4eebfc-75b0-57eb-acfb-cae4a0c06d39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505327} {:answer/id #uuid "cec8de79-540b-5e73-9891-cd653f228d9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505501} {:answer/id #uuid "d5fe4910-17a2-5f7b-b1a8-694b1e285a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-x}{1+x})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e4eebfc-75b0-57eb-acfb-cae4a0c06d39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505327} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b4efcc4-5cc4-5cf8-81d6-746ced2d80e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c2e8309b-0e6c-591c-8179-3fd8b7ab6d9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-125981" :question/problem "If $f(x)=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡({x}^{2})$  and $x\\ge 0,$ calculate  ${f}^{-1}(x)$ from first-principles." :question/worked-solution "Let $y=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡{x}^{2}.$ Note that $f(x)$ is a one-to-one function on $x\\ge 0.$ We have $y\\in [0,1)$ for $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ \n\nTo find the inverse function we swap $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in [0,1)$ and $y\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of $\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{e}^{2{y}^{2}}-1}{{e}^{2{y}^{2}}+1} \\\\ x({e}^{2{y}^{2}}+1) & ={e}^{2{y}^{2}}-1 \\\\ (x-1){e}^{2{y}^{2}} & +(x+1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the ${e}^{2{y}^{2}}$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2{y}^{2}} & =-\\frac{x+1}{x-1} \\\\ {e}^{2{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{1+x}{1-x} \\\\ 2{y}^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}) \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y\\ge 0,$ we select the positive root. Therefore,\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505826 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60753404-5bba-5ada-91bb-6605d38b24e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505299}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60753404-5bba-5ada-91bb-6605d38b24e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505299} :answer-field/id #uuid "99bf3307-f793-5f62-81bd-770491a15c81" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dfca6b72-2e68-5c63-8b6b-82019963b193" :question/math-academy-id "q-351931" :question/problem "Find the coefficient of $(x-1)^{2}$ in the second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x+2}$ at $x=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe coefficient of $(x-1)^{2}$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The second-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2}(x-1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $(x-1)^{2}$ in the above Taylor polynomial is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2}.\n$$\n\nTo find this coefficient, we start by computing the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{x+2}=(x+2)^{-1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-(x+2)^{-2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2(x+2)^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate ${f}^{″}(x)$ at $x=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(1)=2(1+2)^{-3}=\\frac{2}{27}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine the coefficient of $(x-1)^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{″}(1)}{2}=\\frac{(\\frac{2}{27})}{2}=\\frac{1}{27}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505828 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5976a6b-1d1d-57ce-8564-63c89f695029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5976a6b-1d1d-57ce-8564-63c89f695029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505555} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5cb8799-b859-5b82-be20-c6134d8d1332" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e2444a41-f0c0-50e5-91b8-38bd6aeb8a43" :question/math-academy-id "q-347942" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)}{{sin}^{4}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)}{{sin}^{4}⁡(3x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{4}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{5}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{18}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /6}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{5}⁡(3x)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{u}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{{u}^{-4}}{-4}){|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}(\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}){|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}(\\frac{1}{{1}^{4}}-{(\\frac{2}{1})}^{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}(1-16) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "210bc91a-0886-5112-af4a-e207417a27a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+\\sqrt{2})t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505159} {:answer/id #uuid "42566f74-ff91-595b-a775-feb8083bfe0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505236} {:answer/id #uuid "5ff4e27e-6481-5c9b-833b-fc99bca024b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505298} {:answer/id #uuid "75971b4e-945e-5996-88b2-e5e64c3d4697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505344} {:answer/id #uuid "f4709c83-7348-56c6-8fb5-f7518f7b7264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ff4e27e-6481-5c9b-833b-fc99bca024b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505298} :answer-field/id #uuid "f35fe716-2eba-5add-9591-f3a00949c2d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fdaedc69-27ce-51b2-8939-51c5e1dbf0b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-125843" :question/problem "$$\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}),$ for $t>0?$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t})}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}+1}{4{t}^{2}}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1}{4{t}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{2t})}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+|\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{2t}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0,$ we have $\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{2t}>0,$ and we can remove the absolute value in the above expression. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{2t}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2{t}^{2}}{2t}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506509 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27b15c20-ae96-5510-bdf6-238327aebb07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505977} {:answer/id #uuid "9420b75f-9ddc-56f2-8501-2ed81e404fc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506163} {:answer/id #uuid "9fa83dc4-ef7c-5fb9-9ca5-7ad2f609a832" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506198} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa37242-7820-582d-b32a-092b97e43ab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506282} {:answer/id #uuid "f9347296-ee2c-5eec-91b9-fff04cdeed07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27b15c20-ae96-5510-bdf6-238327aebb07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505977} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2b45ba0-9d41-52b1-8d31-cba0ed77bf02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06abf62c-6dfa-52f6-818c-3858ac967b34" :question/math-academy-id "q-138850" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.$  The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{({x}^{2}+2x)^{5}}{3\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡x}}((ax+36)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(b{x}^{2}+cx)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+2x)^{6})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1/3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2x}\\cdot (2x+2)+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{3}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\frac{12(x+1)}{{x}^{2}+2x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2x)^{6}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\frac{36(x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3({x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+2x)^{5}}{3\\sqrt[3]{{sin}^{2}⁡x}}((36x+36)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(1{x}^{2}+2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=36+1+2=39.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506513 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39eac370-b597-5b13-a139-ed248af8a490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506009} {:answer/id #uuid "3e37cd6e-52b0-57de-acb7-d9a7a4ef0fe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506014} {:answer/id #uuid "6c234f23-48fe-556c-a0b1-24c477bfbdfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506100} {:answer/id #uuid "d2bacdde-7997-50ac-944f-7a50e4a49e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506288} {:answer/id #uuid "deac3608-20ae-52c0-97b0-276c7fcb4d55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2bacdde-7997-50ac-944f-7a50e4a49e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506288} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3dff71e-7253-5db5-a8db-526f8787e942" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11c48698-c6fd-5725-a4b0-1737e6fdc309" :question/math-academy-id "q-138831" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}.$  The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}((ax+45)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(4{x}^{2}+bx+c)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product, quotient, and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+6)^{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({cos}^{4}⁡x)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x-1)^{7}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+6}+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-7\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-1} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{3}{x+6}-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{7}{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{3}{x+6}-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{7}{x-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}(\\frac{3}{x+6}-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{7}{x-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}(\\frac{3(x-1)-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x(x+6)(x-1)-7(x+6)}{(x+6)(x-1)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+6)^{3}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{7}}(\\frac{-4x-45-4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{2}+5x-6)}{(x+6)(x-1)}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{4}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}(4x+45+4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{2}+5x-6)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}((4x+45)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{2}+5x-6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x+6)^{2}{cos}^{3}⁡x}{(x-1)^{8}}((4x+45)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(4{x}^{2}+20x-24))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=4+20+(-24)=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506518 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1424a6ad-ac38-52fa-bea7-f95d3db15e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14x^2+63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1424a6ad-ac38-52fa-bea7-f95d3db15e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14x^2+63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505950} :answer-field/id #uuid "61e10619-0457-5f47-8c3c-d968af092244" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "19b1b664-4a0c-5ebc-8d57-f886d4c9f0c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-343952" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt[4]{\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{8}}},$ find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n| ${y}^{′}=$ | $-$ | $x($\n{{field-1}}$)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $2(2{x}^{2}+1)^{3}\\sqrt[4]{({x}^{2}+4)^{3}}$ |" :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[4]{\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{8}}} \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{8}}]}^{1/4} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4}(2{x}^{2}+1)^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4}(2{x}^{2}+1)^{-2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((2{x}^{2}+1)^{-2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}+1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+4}\\cdot (2x)-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot (4x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{x}{2({x}^{2}+4)}-\\frac{8x}{2{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}-\\frac{16x}{2{x}^{2}+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{y}{2}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}-\\frac{16x}{2{x}^{2}+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt[4]{\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{8}}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt[4]{\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)^{8}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}-\\frac{16x}{2{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4}}{2(2{x}^{2}+1)^{2}}(\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}-\\frac{16x}{2{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4}}{2(2{x}^{2}+1)^{2}}(\\frac{x(2{x}^{2}+1)-16x({x}^{2}+4)}{({x}^{2}+4)(2{x}^{2}+1)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{1/4}}{2(2{x}^{2}+1)^{2}}(\\frac{-14{x}^{3}-63x}{({x}^{2}+4)(2{x}^{2}+1)}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x(14{x}^{2}+63)}{2(2{x}^{2}+1)^{3}\\sqrt[4]{({x}^{2}+4)^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506525 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fc9c0e4-94e6-5a58-b402-1569ccdf0546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p+q=\\frac{19}{4},\\,z=-4$ and $z=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506114} {:answer/id #uuid "854c086c-10d6-59fc-a728-734ed635b82f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p+q=-\\frac{19}{4},\\,z=4$ and $z=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506144} {:answer/id #uuid "9dedfe61-c595-5e0a-ab8d-634757cce0c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p+q=\\frac{25}{4},\\,z=-4$ and $z=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506189} {:answer/id #uuid "a3b96d21-d357-5674-892a-42feaf9e8b50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p+q=\\frac{3}{4},\\,z=4$ and $z=-7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506208} {:answer/id #uuid "f0eb231a-0f38-50bf-8cf9-3768fa9ab226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p+q=\\frac{19}{4},\\,z=4$ and $z=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0eb231a-0f38-50bf-8cf9-3768fa9ab226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p+q=\\frac{19}{4},\\,z=4$ and $z=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506356} :answer-field/id #uuid "9418bd8b-7dee-502f-b277-9872940fd25e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2236615a-502f-5e6f-8a3b-d0bb2e05f4c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-35049" :question/problem "The equation $3{z}^{2}-12=9z$ can be written in the form $(z+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $\\,p+q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{2}-12 & =9z \\\\ 3{z}^{2}-9z-12 & =0 \\\\ 3({z}^{2}-3z-4) & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2}-3z-4 & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2}-2\\cdot z\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} & =4 \\\\ {z}^{2}-2\\cdot z\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =-4 \\\\ [{z}^{2}-2\\cdot z\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}]-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =-4 \\\\ {(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{4} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=-\\frac{3}{2},q=\\frac{25}{4}$ and $p+q=\\frac{19}{4}.$\n\nNow, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4}} \\\\ |z-\\frac{3}{2}| & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ z-\\frac{3}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ z & =\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}=4 \\\\ z & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{5}{2}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $z=4$ and $z=-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506527 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "419792fe-6389-5363-a771-125381c66945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x^3+8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "419792fe-6389-5363-a771-125381c66945" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x^3+8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506021} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1cca9dd-6ad8-57a8-a947-88e8f34aa52d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "254041d3-aef5-5074-80f4-67b8b1344bc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-312672" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\sqrt{\\frac{{({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)}^{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}},$ find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}($\n{{field-1}}$+\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8))$" :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)}^{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)}^{2}}}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{1/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{1/2}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{1/2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}\\cdot (4{x}^{3}+4x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{4{x}^{3}+4x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{y}{2}(\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt{\\frac{{({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)}^{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)}^{2}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{|{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8|}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}(\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8>0\\text{ }∀x\\in R$, so $|{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8|={x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8$ and we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}(\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}[\\frac{8{x}^{3}+8x}{{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8}\\cdot ({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}(8{x}^{3}+8x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+8))\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506532 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ebc9a01-e27f-509b-a68a-42b56dc2ad7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\ln(6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ebc9a01-e27f-509b-a68a-42b56dc2ad7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\ln(6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506045} :answer-field/id #uuid "7487540a-070b-5d24-9ba2-dc636972e043" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506432} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec54cd01-acca-5a25-833a-60eab750baf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec54cd01-acca-5a25-833a-60eab750baf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506345} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0efc8ad-b4c8-5f06-a724-3416448817ff" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506453} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97af42c5-8f26-50fc-a29e-fb1a80f1a04f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97af42c5-8f26-50fc-a29e-fb1a80f1a04f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506175} :answer-field/id #uuid "d150bf24-0895-5aa8-98a7-c4a97ebe264b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "310e9298-b624-54d9-ac03-bed77b7fdecc" :question/math-academy-id "q-343350" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}.$  Find ${y}^{′}.$\n\n| ${y}^{′}$ | $=$ | ${6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,($\n{{field-1}}$(x-2)(3-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$x\\,-$\n{{field-3}}$)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $(x-2)^{5}(3-x)^{4}$ |" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient, product, and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x-2)^{4})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((3-x)^{3})) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2))-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-2}-3\\cdot \\frac{-1}{3-x} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{4}{x-2}+\\frac{3}{3-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{4}{x-2}+\\frac{3}{3-x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}(-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{4}{x-2}+\\frac{3}{3-x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}(\\frac{-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)(x-2)(3-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4(3-x)+3(x-2)}{(x-2)(3-x)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{(x-2)^{4}(3-x)^{3}}(\\frac{-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6)(x-2)(3-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+7x-18}{(x-2)(3-x)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡6(x-2)(3-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+7x-18)}{(x-2)^{5}(3-x)^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506534 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03e5c2fc-62c3-5cff-8c3f-12578ac7eec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505922} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd9e9ba-35f5-5dc8-b23d-1b83df5ca67c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.5\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506037} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad7d797-78b2-528c-9d9b-806dc635caa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506068} {:answer/id #uuid "8c834b34-6910-5d98-ab6c-76660ea17f16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.5\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506154} {:answer/id #uuid "a3ab0c31-9934-5df4-8de8-4658ed227b1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3ab0c31-9934-5df4-8de8-4658ed227b1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506207} :answer-field/id #uuid "893ce6e9-d2a5-5687-890a-43b20d335c93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3340fda4-31bb-56af-bac8-5dcbd67440f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-116730" :question/problem "A spherical hot air balloon is inflated. At a particular moment, the rate of increase in the volume of the balloon is numerically equal to nine-halfs the rate of increase in its surface area. What is the radius of the balloon at that moment?" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the balloon, $V$ the volume, and $A$ its surface area. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{9}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the volume $V$ and the surface area $A$ of the sphere. Since $A=4\\pi {r}^{2}$ and $V=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{r}^{3},$ the relationship between $V$ and $A$ is\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{r}^{3}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{(\\frac{A}{4\\pi })}^{3/2}=\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{3(4\\pi )^{1/2}}=\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}A}(\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{{A}^{1/2}}{4\\sqrt{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{9}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{{A}^{1/2}}{4\\sqrt{\\pi }} \\\\ {A}^{1/2} & =18\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\ A & ={(18\\sqrt{\\pi })}^{2} \\\\ A & =324\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $A=4\\pi {r}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\pi {r}^{2} & =324\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =81 \\\\ r & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506535 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7482730a-2ef8-51f5-a35d-2f7208beda4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7482730a-2ef8-51f5-a35d-2f7208beda4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506120} :answer-field/id #uuid "22d1d24c-d1f3-5b29-bd9f-d25502738b67" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "37194e35-397f-5f0f-b8ce-0e1f1331cb3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-344726" :question/problem "![](images/1c/1c16496b13f9ad9fc995cc59dedf1777f25e394ca61dd2ea493d8bd8f69c5e14.png)\n\nPaint dripping onto a floor forms a circular puddle. The radius of the puddle increases at a rate of $10\\,\\text{in/min}.$ How fast is the area of the puddle increasing at the moment when the radius is $\\frac{9}{4\\pi }\\,\\text{in}?$\n\nThe area of the puddle is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$" :question/worked-solution "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=\\frac{9}{4\\pi },$ and we are also told that $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=10.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the area formula for the circle and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=10$ and $r=\\frac{9}{4\\pi },$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =10\\cdot 2\\pi (\\frac{9}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the puddle is increasing at a rate of $45\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506542 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12d9ae4e-8ee9-5dab-a678-998b84633eec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/14/1443d401eb2bd5a6a699f6c80fdd33f95270bed6e24d55165aff7b90bbd546b2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505947} {:answer/id #uuid "2d9ef012-bf37-5052-b96d-18f78871459b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/eb/ebda9e955c2613aa9e65b950ac80ad7ec4e52d6db6fdcce76cc2f89bbc20b0d4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505993} {:answer/id #uuid "364c3910-7df3-5411-bad0-d653131067da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7d/7d2e6671729b92d2ac42a357268a6545f1d3491c7c1a6133e7e460227c8a04da.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506003} {:answer/id #uuid "93ee5e59-9e5d-5c6a-8a09-ad948704dae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/35/3589e407d3ff892f4bfb00e73cf0c5e77a7fb4aaee39ecc3daeca53b60b49773.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506162} {:answer/id #uuid "99281185-8a83-594a-94a5-070bba47ed34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/94/944ad59d879c9eb7fbf08615659d5ac6a4b88ca37cb2dda50ccad7aa3f28046b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d9ef012-bf37-5052-b96d-18f78871459b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/eb/ebda9e955c2613aa9e65b950ac80ad7ec4e52d6db6fdcce76cc2f89bbc20b0d4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505993} :answer-field/id #uuid "f308d5d5-0b30-5d3f-8d6d-a8777cb225e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5f4cf73b-e442-544c-845a-c7e6521bdf69" :question/math-academy-id "q-155556" :question/problem "Represent the solution of the inequality $-\\frac{a}{4}>-2$ on a number line." :question/worked-solution "The variable $a$ is being *divided* by $4.$ To isolate $a$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-4.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{a}{4}\\,\\, & >\\,\\,-2 \\\\ (-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{a}{4})\\,\\, & <\\,\\,(-4)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ (-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{a}{4})\\,\\, & <\\,\\,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{a}{4}) & <8 \\\\ (\\cancel{-}4)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}\\frac{a}{4}) & <8 \\\\ 4\\cdot \\frac{a}{4} & <8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $4$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot \\frac{a}{4} & <8 \\\\ \\frac{4a}{4} & <8 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{4}a}{\\cancel{4}} & <8 \\\\ a & <8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a<8.$ The solution can be expressed on a number line, as shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/eb/ebda9e955c2613aa9e65b950ac80ad7ec4e52d6db6fdcce76cc2f89bbc20b0d4.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506548 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d50330c-784c-5a84-ac1e-5c7c5be92530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{3}{4},\\,x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x=\\frac{13}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505935} {:answer/id #uuid "2393a4d4-762d-5560-9226-5a0e216dec51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{3}{4},\\,x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505969} {:answer/id #uuid "8910933f-a80b-5b82-961d-2627b5f5280c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{3}{2},\\,x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506150} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ee5ca9-ea89-54cd-839a-29262cd0a71a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{3}{2},\\,x=-1$ and $x=-\\frac{5}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506213} {:answer/id #uuid "f62dae0d-aa09-5a1e-b79c-fc4344431af4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{3}{4},\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{3}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2393a4d4-762d-5560-9226-5a0e216dec51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{3}{4},\\,x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505969} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcb7d856-8042-5bbe-be63-7001e1b25a2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506486}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6c6b5462-ae7e-592e-8c21-3452425e7810" :question/math-academy-id "q-60363" :question/problem "The equation $-4{x}^{2}-6x+10=0$ can be written in the form $4(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{x}^{2}-6x+10) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x-10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+6x-10 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x & =10 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x & =10 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x) & =10 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}x+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}] & =10 \\\\ 4[{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{16}] & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{9}{16} & =10 \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4} & =10 \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =10+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{49}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{3}{4}.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{49}{4} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{49}{16} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{49}{16}} \\\\ |x+\\frac{3}{4}| & =\\frac{7}{4} \\\\ x+\\frac{3}{4} & =\\pm \\frac{7}{4} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\pm \\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}=-\\frac{10}{4}=-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{7}{4}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $x=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506552 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04c28e5d-0de9-502d-81aa-7351f4920069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505924} {:answer/id #uuid "90c27865-1c0b-519b-81bb-de523a0ce9d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506157} {:answer/id #uuid "93851dca-79be-57a5-b496-18ef7187487f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506161} {:answer/id #uuid "b193db5a-f715-5820-8744-ed3301ee43c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506234} {:answer/id #uuid "ebacda43-d38a-518b-8124-0083b7f84186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebacda43-d38a-518b-8124-0083b7f84186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506342} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d436c14-aa02-5c1c-8f96-e30ae5edad2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "76085196-6a2f-5c76-90cb-5c742aa98a2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-138849" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}(x-3)^{8}}.$ The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}(x-3)^{3}(a{x}^{2}+bx+c).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}(x-3)^{8}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt{(x-3)^{8}} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)^{3/2}(x-3)^{8/2} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)^{3/2}(x-3)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+1)^{3/2}(x-3)^{4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+1)^{3/2})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x-3)^{4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot 2x+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-3} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{3x}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{4}{x-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{3x}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{4}{x-3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}(x-3)^{8}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}(x-3)^{8}}(\\frac{3x}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{4}{x-3}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}(x-3)^{8}}(\\frac{3x(x-3)+4({x}^{2}+1)}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-3)}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}(x-3)^{8}}(\\frac{7{x}^{2}-9x+4}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-3)}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)^{3/2}(x-3)^{4}(\\frac{7{x}^{2}-9x+4}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-3)}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+1)^{1/2}(x-3)^{3}(7{x}^{2}-9x+4) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}(x-3)^{3}(7{x}^{2}-9x+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=7+(-9)+4=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506564 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "102790ff-7839-5d1c-a7c1-c89c090c0d2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505942}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "102790ff-7839-5d1c-a7c1-c89c090c0d2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505942} :answer-field/id #uuid "274547bb-be34-5b65-bb9d-f32876fabd37" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506398} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4323142-e936-5cd3-b6de-3092378faa67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4323142-e936-5cd3-b6de-3092378faa67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506359} :answer-field/id #uuid "94665d5f-2175-540b-accc-e742e344e1b0" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506448} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5274db64-5e9a-58d1-a778-b4f7735c7525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5274db64-5e9a-58d1-a778-b4f7735c7525" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506050} :answer-field/id #uuid "b03d9a12-bbbf-5ee1-a420-92022427414d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8a442047-ed6e-5bb4-89b9-beaee4b1e553" :question/math-academy-id "q-338291" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}.$  Find the derivative of $y.$\n\n| ${y}^{′}$ | $=$ | $(x+2)^{3}\\,(-5{x}^{3}\\,-$\n{{field-1}}${x}^{2}\\,+$ \n{{field-2}}$x\\,-$\n{{field-3}}$)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | ${({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{4}$ |" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+2)^{4})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}+2x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2))-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}+2x-1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+2}-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+2x-1}\\cdot (3{x}^{2}+2) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{4}{x+2}-\\frac{3(3{x}^{2}+2)}{{x}^{3}+2x-1} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{4}{x+2}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}+6}{{x}^{3}+2x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{4}{x+2}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}+6}{{x}^{3}+2x-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}(\\frac{4}{x+2}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}+6}{{x}^{3}+2x-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}(\\frac{4({x}^{3}+2x-1)-(x+2)(9{x}^{2}+6)}{(x+2)({x}^{3}+2x-1)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)^{4}}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{3}}(\\frac{-5{x}^{3}-18{x}^{2}+2x-16}{(x+2)({x}^{3}+2x-1)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)^{3}(-5{x}^{3}-18{x}^{2}+2x-16)}{{({x}^{3}+2x-1)}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506577 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24dcf1bc-40b1-5496-a498-3a7dae4ffe85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "271" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505972} {:answer/id #uuid "94c691dd-0578-5e12-9cc1-90a7a7bb4085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506165} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f872ba-2453-55d3-97e1-d1431eaa6668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506204} {:answer/id #uuid "e2265b1b-5fa7-5e22-a840-d2ec09a5ad1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "321" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506326} {:answer/id #uuid "eef1c07b-39fe-56ff-ad05-22dd4b396724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eef1c07b-39fe-56ff-ad05-22dd4b396724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506349} :answer-field/id #uuid "13bb00dc-f20f-5ab2-8e37-6185dcb84a29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "adde1803-10ec-5c3d-8c04-d7e2c5d99587" :question/math-academy-id "q-138830" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}.$ The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{(x+3)^{5}(x+8)^{4}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}(ax+63-8({x}^{2}+bx+c)\\operatorname{cot}⁡x).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the product, quotient, and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+3)^{6})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+8)^{5})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({sin}^{8}⁡x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+8)-8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+3)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+8)-8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+3}+5\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+8}-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{6}{x+3}+\\frac{5}{x+8}-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{6}{x+3}+\\frac{5}{x+8}-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}(\\frac{6}{x+3}+\\frac{5}{x+8}-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}(\\frac{6(x+8)+5(x+3)-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x(x+3)(x+8)}{(x+3)(x+8)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}(\\frac{6x+48+5x+15-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x({x}^{2}+11x+24)}{(x+3)(x+8)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{6}(x+8)^{5}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}(\\frac{11x+63-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x({x}^{2}+11x+24)}{(x+3)(x+8)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{5}(x+8)^{4}}{{sin}^{8}⁡x}(11x+63-8\\operatorname{cot}⁡x({x}^{2}+11x+24))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=11+11+24=46.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506582 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ab36fdc-7bd5-5430-8835-a0815a440065" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505956} {:answer/id #uuid "6d88a707-e10c-5afc-bea5-52cb9dd63b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506103} {:answer/id #uuid "7e987b10-51ae-5db1-b678-922798639d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506131} {:answer/id #uuid "9515e64c-16bf-58d0-b027-ae01ea34515a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506166} {:answer/id #uuid "d0164b4c-0fc5-5386-8a13-5df40b0b0015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0164b4c-0fc5-5386-8a13-5df40b0b0015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506284} :answer-field/id #uuid "de6a18ff-6676-56f5-b05e-7d4da313f1e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506489}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bc8a78e5-ffc8-5ad6-abfc-11fb82c73f1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-138865" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}}.$ The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{a{x}^{5}+3{x}^{4}+b{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+c}{({x}^{2}+2x+2)^{3/2}\\sqrt{{x}^{4}+2x}}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{4}+2x}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2x+2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{4}+2x)^{1/2}}{({x}^{2}+2x+2)^{1/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{({x}^{4}+2x)^{1/2}}{({x}^{2}+2x+2)^{1/2}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{4}+2x)^{1/2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+2x+2)^{1/2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+2x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{4}+2x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2x+2)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{4}+2x}\\cdot (4{x}^{3}+2)-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}\\cdot (2x+2) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{2{x}^{3}+1}{{x}^{4}+2x}-\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{2{x}^{3}+1}{{x}^{4}+2x}-\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+2x+2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}}(\\frac{2{x}^{3}+1}{{x}^{4}+2x}-\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}}(\\frac{(2{x}^{3}+1)({x}^{2}+2x+2)-(x+1)({x}^{4}+2x)}{({x}^{4}+2x)({x}^{2}+2x+2)}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}}(\\frac{2{x}^{5}+4{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+2x+2-({x}^{5}+{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+2x)}{({x}^{4}+2x)({x}^{2}+2x+2)}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{2}+2x+2}}(\\frac{{x}^{5}+3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+2}{({x}^{4}+2x)({x}^{2}+2x+2)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1{x}^{5}+3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+2}{({x}^{2}+2x+2)^{3/2}\\sqrt{{x}^{4}+2x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+4+2=7.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "202f1ef1-7013-51df-9e16-c9120bd4144f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505965} {:answer/id #uuid "5e31ff2b-eed8-54a4-9c63-1385779eeb3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506077} {:answer/id #uuid "838261f2-b5a9-5711-a5d7-9938944a3fc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of intersection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506140} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e52d3b-1bd1-557a-a8af-a88e19d06865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506209} {:answer/id #uuid "ddadc2d4-3fc4-5e73-bbc6-8b7b6bb74343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "838261f2-b5a9-5711-a5d7-9938944a3fc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of intersection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506140} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ac868f5-8945-532d-ae24-0a2c85097e29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c693f3f3-9251-58ce-8006-d594413ed6b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-8010" :question/problem "Calculate the $x$-coordinates of the points where the hyperbola $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-{y}^{2}=1$ intersects the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x,$ we first replace $y$ with $\\frac{1}{3}x$ in the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-{(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ 0 & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the last equation is a false statement, we conclude that the hyperbola does not intersect the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506590 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f09fc632-c551-511d-b5ff-0a2ef83dfc99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f09fc632-c551-511d-b5ff-0a2ef83dfc99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506354} :answer-field/id #uuid "70fdc4f0-4261-5d00-b2fa-9b5690965424" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9d699d5-e68c-517c-8c66-c0218f7be321" :question/math-academy-id "q-344727" :question/problem "![](images/95/95e09441c0ab651a759862d3689aee517d089a43d2089697a1b77882de1e5d73.png)\n\nWater leaking onto a patio forms a circular pool. The radius of the pool increases at a rate of $3\\,\\text{m/min}.$ How fast is the area of the pool increasing at the moment when the radius is $\\frac{5}{2\\pi }\\,\\text{m}?$\n\nThe area of the pool is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{m}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$" :question/worked-solution "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=\\frac{5}{2\\pi },$ and we are also told that $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=3.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the area formula for the circle and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=3$ and $r=\\frac{5}{2\\pi },$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =3\\cdot 2\\pi (\\frac{5}{2\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the pool is increasing at a rate of $15\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506591 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f59a9ba-364e-508e-a41a-55a558b2bb49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505941} {:answer/id #uuid "478822c2-e030-5e90-884b-19998c062866" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506029} {:answer/id #uuid "75b16ee9-4f9f-5d41-b9b6-01fc0fada42c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506121} {:answer/id #uuid "9e556be2-fc3f-5682-8727-573dea82908b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506192} {:answer/id #uuid "cb83d552-a42d-5c11-aa25-bd36f59bc3ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f59a9ba-364e-508e-a41a-55a558b2bb49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505941} :answer-field/id #uuid "63b209a8-1c66-5330-a9ec-0e55e08cba8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dbe4c4ed-7bf7-5234-8564-800989eb5aa4" :question/math-academy-id "q-138839" :question/problem "Consider the function $y=\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}}}{{e}^{2-4x}}.$ The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}-1}}{{e}^{2-4x}}((x+2)^{2}+g(x)).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We have the function \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}}}{{e}^{2-4x}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}}}{{e}^{2-4x}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x+1)^{{x}^{2}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2-4x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & ={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-(2-4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-2+4x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+1}+4 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}}}{{e}^{2-4x}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}}}{{e}^{2-4x}}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x+1}+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}}}{{e}^{2-4x}}(\\frac{2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}+4(x+1)}{x+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}-1}}{{e}^{2-4x}}(2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}+4(x+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}-1}}{{e}^{2-4x}}(2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+{x}^{2}+4x+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{{x}^{2}-1}}{{e}^{2-4x}}(2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+(x+2)^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x)=2x(x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507404 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22440f7b-035c-540b-b0e2-3dcd53956334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506747} {:answer/id #uuid "24264748-c5f8-54b5-aa97-cacdbd3e54a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506756} {:answer/id #uuid "9798202a-5866-5647-b043-5b3551c6a2a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507008} {:answer/id #uuid "baca3216-958b-5e79-b70f-2b61c19055ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507089} {:answer/id #uuid "c1195dcf-977f-50d8-932c-4e76e5fdb1c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24264748-c5f8-54b5-aa97-cacdbd3e54a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506756} :answer-field/id #uuid "b73f9e06-0243-5c21-9043-a7def06f5f6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "14f47d14-d694-51ea-abc7-d932943c0b1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-44172" :question/problem "Factor ${x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=\\frac{1}{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507408 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b2b3346-7673-59b0-8162-666645b193ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b2b3346-7673-59b0-8162-666645b193ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506854} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b0ffb3a-4b6c-5434-a8c2-b27219cc6547" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1f0168e5-5e69-5504-a3b6-aca93e900a7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-344924" :question/problem "![](images/d1/d1d974b84be93c1178820f27c40f7b2bfae66837f5f64dcb7353f968abad966c.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(1)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$\n\n${h}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(1)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(1)=2$ and $g(1)=1.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-1,3)$ equals $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${f}^{′}(1)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (0,3)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${g}^{′}(1)=-1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-1)}{(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{3}{2}}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507419 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3baa451a-05ef-5ea6-9e67-38333ef4f803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\ge -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506819} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad18f48-f87f-5a8e-83ca-7b7baaf8d85b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\ge 14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506892} {:answer/id #uuid "9976ab5e-8eac-58d3-a410-a11083f1fd8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507012} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ea89bc-8d49-5eb8-bdf1-af8685391da2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\le 14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507027} {:answer/id #uuid "ece206a6-bb4a-5ca0-a27c-6ac1106fcba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\le -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9976ab5e-8eac-58d3-a410-a11083f1fd8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507012} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd44771e-0700-538a-9998-e17199c5831d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "37bbccda-0b14-5e04-9a10-40c81c14c83e" :question/math-academy-id "q-156048" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{2y-8}{3}\\le \\frac{8-2y}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality by the lowest common positive multiple of the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y-8}{3} & \\le \\frac{8-2y}{4} \\\\ 12\\cdot (\\frac{2y-8}{3}) & \\le 12\\cdot (\\frac{8-2y}{4}) \\\\ 4\\cdot (2y-8) & \\le 3\\cdot (8-2y) \\\\ 8y-32 & \\le 24-6y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8y-32 & \\le 24-6y \\\\ 8y-32+6y & \\le 24-6y+6y \\\\ 14y-32 & \\le 24 \\\\ 14y-32+32 & \\le 24+32 \\\\ 14y & \\le 56 \\\\ \\frac{14y}{14} & \\le \\frac{56}{14} \\\\ y & \\le 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y\\le 4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507426 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9b0d25f-a9be-59cb-9e73-c90c91605ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9b0d25f-a9be-59cb-9e73-c90c91605ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507052} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f3c02df-5522-58ab-abd4-e9b78610a514" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507295} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cdcb9fbd-5555-5112-b178-4b472e36dad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdcb9fbd-5555-5112-b178-4b472e36dad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507126} :answer-field/id #uuid "719477eb-00fb-512e-85cd-7a35def7de91" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41e3b8c9-bf8c-5f28-a82e-0ab2c9c99da3" :question/math-academy-id "q-181364" :question/problem "Find the solution to the inequality $\\frac{1-7x}{5}\\ge 2.$\n\nThe solution is $x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-7x}{5} & \\ge 2 \\\\ 5\\cdot (\\frac{1-7x}{5}) & \\ge 5\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\cancel{5}\\cdot (\\frac{1-7x}{\\cancel{5}}) & \\ge 10 \\\\ 1-7x & \\ge 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles.\n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-7x & \\ge 10 \\\\ 1-7x-1 & \\ge 10-1 \\\\ -7x & \\ge 9 \\\\ \\frac{-7x}{-7} & \\,\\,\\le \\,\\,\\frac{9}{-7} \\\\ x & \\le -\\frac{9}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\le -\\frac{9}{7}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507442 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31315a0d-2c0e-5f94-a1ff-4b7faeb27a09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506794} {:answer/id #uuid "3701d3f2-5410-556e-b0a7-75420b9f36b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506810} {:answer/id #uuid "374322ff-4086-5b83-87ee-c3122f61bd52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+3)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506811} {:answer/id #uuid "c0be764b-beb0-5776-8b10-64f11f6978bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507101} {:answer/id #uuid "d9c7dbd4-3ec1-54d0-8413-03bba2ac8751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31315a0d-2c0e-5f94-a1ff-4b7faeb27a09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506794} :answer-field/id #uuid "8adb6eba-9169-5d39-b01b-4fde7ef436d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e023823-cd56-5877-8c6f-e730b2d09ff6" :question/math-academy-id "q-78924" :question/problem "${x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{9}{16}=$" :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a sum\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2ax+{a}^{2}=(x+a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{9}{16}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}x+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of sum formula with $a=\\frac{3}{4}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d935e86d-2878-5713-88af-732c2cd4dd43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d935e86d-2878-5713-88af-732c2cd4dd43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507145} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb3c213e-a427-5f30-8b6a-3f5985900ded" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62a29958-3d43-5575-a73a-6517a1734f8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-324461" :question/problem "The price of a deli sandwich is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ dollars and a standard deviation of $$1.10.$ In addition, in $90%$ of cases the price is between $$0.80\\mu $ and $$1.20\\mu .$ Find the average price, **rounding your answer to two decimal places**.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.97\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,1.8808\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$\\mu ≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$dollars$" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent a sandwich price. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{1.10}^{2}),$ and we want to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.80\\mu <X<1.20\\mu )=0.90.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.80\\mu <X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.90 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.90 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.45 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.5+0.45 \\\\ P(X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.95.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.20\\mu ) & =0.95 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.20\\mu }{1.10}) & =0.95 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.20\\mu }{1.10}) & =0.95 \\\\ \\frac{0.20\\mu }{1.10} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.95).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.95)=1.6449.\n$$\n\nFinally then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.20\\mu }{1.10} & =1.6449 \\\\ 0.20\\mu  & =1.809\\,39 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{1.809\\,39}{0.20} \\\\  & ≈9.05\\,\\text{dollars}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507452 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33710c61-22d6-514d-a654-305c39257be0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33710c61-22d6-514d-a654-305c39257be0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506801} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dd58159-33f5-5db7-909d-d7a72f3ebd4b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7fe715dd-222c-5303-8e5d-661af680f9ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-314062" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we let\n\n$$\nL={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the formula for the instantaneous rate of change\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a},\n$$\n\nwe see that if we let $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ and $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{f(x)-f\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}}=L.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis confirms that $L={f}^{′}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$\n\nComputing the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\,{f}^{′}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n$$\n\nand therefore, we conclude that $L=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507456 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "686fb959-8e23-5381-8bd9-2f970d41778e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "686fb959-8e23-5381-8bd9-2f970d41778e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506922} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fbfa6a5-6a10-5832-a94e-61877fd05794" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507328} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f5f8125-cec1-5372-9fa6-f054c0ec30d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f5f8125-cec1-5372-9fa6-f054c0ec30d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507020} :answer-field/id #uuid "b06e3cb5-1f20-5499-8cd9-ff51fcfab7bc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "88277403-5532-598b-ac5e-1cdba1716886" :question/math-academy-id "q-181353" :question/problem "Find the solution to the inequality $\\frac{5t-6}{2}\\le 1.$\n\nThe solution is $t$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5t-6}{2}\\le 1 \\\\ 2\\cdot (\\frac{5t-6}{2}) & \\le 2\\cdot 1 \\\\ \\cancel{2}\\cdot (\\frac{5t-6}{\\cancel{2}}) & \\le 2 \\\\ 5t-6 & \\le 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5t-6 & \\le 2 \\\\ 5t-6+6 & \\le 2+6 \\\\ 5t & \\le 8 \\\\ \\frac{5t}{5} & \\le \\frac{8}{5} \\\\ t & \\le \\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $t\\le \\frac{8}{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507457 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46bfa4c3-af30-5756-82bb-059f1b23c4a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506843} {:answer/id #uuid "4850c323-0008-5586-a92c-51d27d9d80e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{25}{6})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506847} {:answer/id #uuid "9af9ba7a-b83f-5100-8ba8-0c8c301f0339" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507013} {:answer/id #uuid "aa4ea64c-142a-5719-b240-6ec5e8ce7a0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{5}{3})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507054} {:answer/id #uuid "b6500cbd-5e5a-59f1-8c25-13b96afe7d98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{5}{3})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46bfa4c3-af30-5756-82bb-059f1b23c4a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506843} :answer-field/id #uuid "f20bb066-c188-50d4-908b-5327e0942d6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8c418bae-22aa-5bc3-86d4-82df570c3162" :question/math-academy-id "q-78927" :question/problem "Factor ${x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{3}x+\\frac{25}{36}.$" :question/worked-solution "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{3}x+\\frac{25}{36}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the constant term as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{3}x+{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{6}x+{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=\\frac{5}{6}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31a95984-f3ff-5d40-aa45-92f765a76484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3s-1}{(s+1)(s-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31a95984-f3ff-5d40-aa45-92f765a76484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3s-1}{(s+1)(s-2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506796} :answer-field/id #uuid "35230cec-1558-5848-ab5a-d11ccbfeb44d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507269} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e846976b-6bbf-5002-a11d-4c4ba08add2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{s+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e846976b-6bbf-5002-a11d-4c4ba08add2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{s+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507190} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6f7f1d9-3783-567e-8823-55b10da69ad9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9a8a2b45-1f2b-5de6-b95b-a5731dcdb607" :question/math-academy-id "q-331914" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=4{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,{X}_{2}(s)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\} &=L\\{-{x}_{1}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\} &=L\\{4{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0) &=-L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0) &=4L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+2L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)+1 &=-{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-3 &=4{X}_{1}(s)+2{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$\n\nNow, solving the first equation for ${X}_{1}(s),$ we get\n\n$$\ns{X}_{1}(s)+1=-{X}_{1}(s)\\,⟹\\,{X}_{1}(s)=-\\frac{1}{s+1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the second equation and solving for ${X}_{2}(s),$ we have\n\n$$\ns{X}_{2}(s)-3=4(-\\frac{1}{s+1})+2{X}_{2}(s)\\,⟹\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{3s-1}{(s+1)(s-2)}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11c2fead-d108-57e7-8db0-525fce50e750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y>-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506713} {:answer/id #uuid "2a9dca56-0354-57b4-a7e8-d9a0ef2a39c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y<-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506778} {:answer/id #uuid "696f454f-a239-53c4-b608-6d6ad371cc2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y<9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506925} {:answer/id #uuid "bd5e7ebb-9abc-5d94-9362-95adeacf2f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y>-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507095} {:answer/id #uuid "e177af7e-0bf6-5205-9657-9a0ed5250651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y>3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11c2fead-d108-57e7-8db0-525fce50e750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y>-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506713} :answer-field/id #uuid "78e5e08f-692a-52d5-9828-56a741cd6532" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9fd93fd8-c1db-5a78-b0d6-62c128a57e89" :question/math-academy-id "q-156057" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{4y-9}{15}<\\frac{y}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality by the lowest common positive multiple of the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4y-9}{15} & <\\frac{y}{3} \\\\ 15\\cdot (\\frac{4y-9}{15}) & <15\\cdot (\\frac{y}{3}) \\\\ 4y-9 & <5y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-9 & <5y \\\\ 4y-9-5y & <5y-5y \\\\ -9-y & <0 \\\\ -9-y+9 & <0+9 \\\\ -y & <9 \\\\ \\frac{-y}{-1} & \\,\\,>\\,\\,\\frac{9}{-1} \\\\ y & >-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y>-9.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507471 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c822341-4e56-50fd-ba09-fc792dcce56b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\frac{1}{2},\\,b=\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506699} {:answer/id #uuid "240cb937-fa6f-5fbd-a8f5-c57634feafc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-\\frac{2}{3},\\,b=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506754} {:answer/id #uuid "2b854fe0-ef1d-5321-90f8-585a9d8b0017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=-\\frac{2}{3},\\,b=-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506780} {:answer/id #uuid "6055db78-569e-51e4-9439-86946d33caf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\frac{3}{2},\\,b=-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506909} {:answer/id #uuid "82cdf392-a488-5d1c-b7fc-d8ed86588e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\frac{3}{2},\\,b=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6055db78-569e-51e4-9439-86946d33caf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a=\\frac{3}{2},\\,b=-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506909} :answer-field/id #uuid "521ab5d3-f6d1-53a5-a68c-844d600c98bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507288}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a26cf28c-df8d-52da-88d5-cd7c098392bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-4594" :question/problem "Find the values of the constants $a$ and $b$ that make the function $f(x)$ below continuous at $x=-1$ and $x=2.$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-5, & x\\le -1 \\\\ 2ax+3b, & -1<x\\le 2 \\\\ 4, & x>2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "At $x=-1,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{-}}f(x) & =f(-1) \\\\  & =-5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(-1)}^{+}}(2ax+3b) \\\\  & =2a(-1)+3b \\\\  & =-2a+3b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) \\\\ -5 & =-2a+3b \\\\ 2a-3b & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, at $x=2,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(2ax+3b) \\\\  & =2a(2)+3b \\\\  & =4a+3b,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}4=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x) \\\\ 4a+3b & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's solve the following system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2a-3b &=5 \\\\ 4a+3b &=4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding both equations we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6a & =9 \\\\ a & =\\frac{9}{6} \\\\ a & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can replace $a$ by $\\frac{3}{2}$ into the first equation. Therefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(\\frac{3}{2})-3b & =5 \\\\ 3-3b & =5 \\\\ -3b & =2 \\\\ b & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$ and $x=2$ for $a=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $b=-\\frac{2}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507475 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b4e37a9-9371-541d-b046-02330a84b683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b4e37a9-9371-541d-b046-02330a84b683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507015} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6100537-9c91-5945-8d50-4bf692d695a1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507346} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "656c88fd-0784-5c15-95e4-e68ff8d0009a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "656c88fd-0784-5c15-95e4-e68ff8d0009a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506918} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae484b73-c158-5737-8b58-c8813ea0f8cf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507350}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a763aa0a-1786-546d-877a-3f8cb8ed771a" :question/math-academy-id "q-181373" :question/problem "Find the solution to the inequality $\\frac{2-3p}{4}<2p+1.$\n\nThe solution is $p$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-3p}{4} & <2p+1 \\\\ 4\\cdot (\\frac{2-3p}{4}) & <4\\cdot (2p+1) \\\\ \\cancel{4}\\cdot (\\frac{2-3p}{\\cancel{4}}) & <4\\cdot (2p+1) \\\\ 2-3p & <8p+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-3p & <8p+4 \\\\ 2-3p-8p & <8p+4-8p \\\\ 2-11p & <4 \\\\ 2-11p-2 & <4-2 \\\\ -11p & <2 \\\\ \\frac{-11p}{-11} & \\,\\,>\\,\\,\\frac{2}{-11} \\\\ p & >-\\frac{2}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $p>-\\frac{2}{11}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507477 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1af436a9-9425-59bc-9879-b5a2d4148878" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a\\le 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506726} {:answer/id #uuid "32818b35-b7cb-56f3-b1f3-4d1137800999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a\\le -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506799} {:answer/id #uuid "63abf159-1bb5-5a42-9daa-463b46621411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a\\ge 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506915} {:answer/id #uuid "a20e1ecc-b32b-5179-94f5-f6fcfeabd193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a\\ge -6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507028} {:answer/id #uuid "fda207e8-8fff-5625-a4ee-cd07bda49aab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a\\ge 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a20e1ecc-b32b-5179-94f5-f6fcfeabd193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a\\ge -6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507028} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ad7f7db-4030-5bba-a9d1-a46ba85090e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a8e1188e-48d5-534b-bcfd-6a2d0af01921" :question/math-academy-id "q-156062" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{2a-4}{8}\\le \\frac{2a}{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality by the lowest common positive multiple of the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2a-4}{8} & \\le \\frac{2a}{6} \\\\ 24\\cdot (\\frac{2a-4}{8}) & \\le 24\\cdot (\\frac{2a}{6}) \\\\ 3\\cdot (2a-4) & \\le 4\\cdot 2a \\\\ 6a-12 & \\le 8a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6a-12 & \\le 8a \\\\ 6a-12-8a & \\le 8a-8a \\\\ -12-2a & \\le 0 \\\\ -12-2a+12 & \\le 0+12 \\\\ -2a & \\le 12 \\\\ \\frac{-2a}{-2} & \\,\\,\\ge \\,\\,\\frac{12}{-2} \\\\ a & \\ge -6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a\\ge -6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507489 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea413de4-f41d-5bd6-8d46-90d7116bd177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "51.99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea413de4-f41d-5bd6-8d46-90d7116bd177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "51.99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507195} :answer-field/id #uuid "abe2d838-2efe-5d60-8dd7-942ce7ac6cc4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd63f548-8bbe-5de7-9356-fc14587f7fab" :question/math-academy-id "q-325117" :question/problem "The heights of oak trees in a park are normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ feet and a standard deviation of $8$ feet. Additionally, $25%$ of the trees are shorter than $45$ feet. Find the percentage of trees taller than $50$ feet. **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z).$*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.4090\\,$ | $\\,0.4052\\,$ | $\\,0.4013\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.4483\\,$ | $\\,0.4443\\,$ | $\\,0.4404\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.0\\,$ | $\\,0.4880\\,$ | $\\,0.4840\\,$ | $\\,0.4801\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of the trees are taller than $50$ feet." :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the height of a randomly selected tree. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{8}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>50).$  \n\nWe know that $P(X<45)=0.25.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<45) & =0.25 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{45-\\mu }{8}) & =0.25 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{45-\\mu }{8}) & =0.25 \\\\ \\frac{45-\\mu }{8} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.25)=-0.6745.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{45-\\mu }{8} & =-0.6745 \\\\ 45-\\mu  & =-5.396 \\\\ \\mu  & =50.396.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(50<X)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>50) & =P(Z>\\frac{50-50.396}{8}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-0.05) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.05) \\\\  & =1-0.4801 \\\\  & ≈51.99%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $51.99%$ of the trees are taller than $50$ feet."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507491 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be4173d4-2fab-56de-9d24-84bcde3b6c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be4173d4-2fab-56de-9d24-84bcde3b6c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507098} :answer-field/id #uuid "25247a53-b3d9-5d93-a5e2-eb10bd145f04" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507262} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04d3bc34-7b3f-57d7-be3b-ca7e45a1f25b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04d3bc34-7b3f-57d7-be3b-ca7e45a1f25b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506685} :answer-field/id #uuid "754b3a47-c518-5c04-b26c-a2ac39a75809" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "beefe539-fabd-5c70-98d6-47fa9012204c" :question/math-academy-id "q-181384" :question/problem "Find the solution to the inequality $\\frac{2z+9}{3}\\ge -z-1.$\n\nThe solution is $z$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $3:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2z+9}{3} & \\ge -z-1 \\\\ 3\\cdot (\\frac{2z+9}{3}) & \\ge 3\\cdot (-z-1) \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot (\\frac{2z+9}{\\cancel{3}}) & \\ge 3\\cdot (-z-1) \\\\ 2z+9 & \\ge -3z-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z+9 & \\ge -3z-3 \\\\ 2z+9+3z & \\ge -3z-3+3z \\\\ 5z+9 & \\ge -3 \\\\ 5z+9-9 & \\ge -3-9 \\\\ 5z & \\ge -12 \\\\ \\frac{5z}{5} & \\ge \\frac{-12}{5} \\\\ z & \\ge -\\frac{12}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z\\ge -\\frac{12}{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507499 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "825a3f94-5d5f-59b8-9d4e-7d0f168885a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z>-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506965} {:answer/id #uuid "9bfb137d-cdc8-5e43-84c6-ea0025793bde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z<24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507016} {:answer/id #uuid "a1a10480-cbf4-58a2-9e6b-17815d58cadd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z<-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507026} {:answer/id #uuid "b207b853-0fed-5188-a900-3bb18b72d272" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z>\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507070} {:answer/id #uuid "ef835f55-6239-5e48-941a-ae73fc43925e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z<-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1a10480-cbf4-58a2-9e6b-17815d58cadd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z<-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507026} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4490670-0ae0-5d23-9e69-8c96fae8a13a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507356}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c8a935c4-ddf1-51df-a83c-2659ff68e2ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-154041" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $4<-\\frac{z}{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "The variable $z$ is being *divided* by $6.$ To isolate $z$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-6$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\,\\, & <\\,\\,-\\frac{z}{6} \\\\ (-6)\\cdot 4\\,\\, & >\\,\\,(-6)\\cdot (-\\frac{z}{6}) \\\\ -24\\,\\, & >\\,\\,(-6)\\cdot (-\\frac{z}{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-24\\,\\, & >(-6)\\cdot (-\\frac{z}{6}) \\\\ -24 & >(\\cancel{-}6)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}\\frac{z}{6}) \\\\ -24 & >6\\cdot \\frac{z}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $6$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-24 & >6\\cdot \\frac{z}{6} \\\\ -24 & >\\frac{6z}{6} \\\\ -24 & >\\frac{\\cancel{6}z}{\\cancel{6}} \\\\ -24 & >z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the inequality with the variable on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nz<-24\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z<-24.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507503 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e90f66c1-50aa-5eec-9fca-bddb4bba7214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e90f66c1-50aa-5eec-9fca-bddb4bba7214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507194} :answer-field/id #uuid "41f396f4-a600-561c-a811-891b140e8ab6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507274} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5197d664-2d80-5cab-8f1a-7a61713e0053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5197d664-2d80-5cab-8f1a-7a61713e0053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506864} :answer-field/id #uuid "835c17f7-36ef-548e-8b46-b6d83fc2e63a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf4150db-c6d9-5a42-a567-768a11a0a924" :question/math-academy-id "q-171616" :question/problem "Find the solution to the inequality $\\frac{5z+2}{3}\\ge 4.$\n\n$z$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5z+2}{3} & \\ge 4 \\\\ 3\\cdot (\\frac{5z+2}{3}) & \\ge 3\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot (\\frac{5z+2}{\\cancel{3}}) & \\ge 12 \\\\ 5z+2 & \\ge 12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5z+2 & \\ge 12 \\\\ 5z+2-2 & \\ge 12-2 \\\\ 5z & \\ge 10 \\\\ \\frac{5z}{5} & \\ge \\frac{10}{5} \\\\ z & \\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z\\ge 2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507506 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adfdc82e-f387-5cca-a236-db6c3b237ae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adfdc82e-f387-5cca-a236-db6c3b237ae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507062} :answer-field/id #uuid "909bfc96-ecbc-5d98-91c1-caba1a5f0edc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507330} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ced4f854-67c0-55a7-8eb2-2342cefa6036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ced4f854-67c0-55a7-8eb2-2342cefa6036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507128} :answer-field/id #uuid "e46faa19-8528-52c0-9d68-d774c89993c7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d5194de3-9348-5e70-86f0-2dc5eae75238" :question/math-academy-id "q-181399" :question/problem "Find the solution to the inequality $\\frac{y+2}{3}<\\frac{y+3}{2}.$\n\nThe solution is $y$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality by the lowest common positive multiple of the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+2}{3} & <\\frac{y+3}{2} \\\\ 6\\cdot (\\frac{y+2}{3}) & <6\\cdot (\\frac{y+3}{2}) \\\\ 2\\cdot (y+2) & <3\\cdot (y+3) \\\\ 2y+4 & <3y+9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+4 & <3y+9 \\\\ 2y+4-3y & <3y+9-3y \\\\ 4-y & <9 \\\\ 4-y-4 & <9-4 \\\\ -y & <5 \\\\ \\frac{-y}{-1} & \\,\\,>\\,\\,\\frac{5}{-1} \\\\ y & >-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y>-5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507508 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4163fb4e-1673-56b1-9e81-b3f9b76bdcdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506831} {:answer/id #uuid "b513ed2d-2ca6-556b-9cdc-932a781ed446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507077} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc323db-b9be-5ba3-89bf-74f4864af6f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507097} {:answer/id #uuid "ef817cb8-b7c8-5338-9ccb-b25eb7ba3fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b513ed2d-2ca6-556b-9cdc-932a781ed446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507077} :answer-field/id #uuid "955e89a0-7f01-5af1-93d8-b8760f8a0f25" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507334} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29bab42e-19fd-5999-bca5-a7f709334255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506773} {:answer/id #uuid "7380ffe2-b480-549e-b9ff-55f23f096330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9x}{8\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506943} {:answer/id #uuid "c1abe79a-867b-5b61-b180-db62e3c89468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9x}{32\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507105} {:answer/id #uuid "db644471-744d-5974-86ca-8c2d68c380d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29bab42e-19fd-5999-bca5-a7f709334255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506773} :answer-field/id #uuid "e28d2851-cb92-5497-aeb3-e6113495aa58" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d683633f-d60f-57e1-b38c-1931a877a7fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-343914" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x),$ where the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $6$-periodic function $(L=3)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2$, we have ${\\omega }_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{2}=\\omega ,$ the $n=2$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=2$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x)=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}))+x(-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″} & =-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{2}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,[-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}]+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})=-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A &=-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{4\\pi }{3}B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=\\frac{9}{16\\pi } \\\\ B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=\\frac{9x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507512 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec708962-ad6c-5d78-85a9-f0cb925fa107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\ln\\left(\\frac{9}{7}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec708962-ad6c-5d78-85a9-f0cb925fa107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\ln\\left(\\frac{9}{7}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507201} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b282f9b-5bb1-5f62-87e7-540c34e64d8c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9591085-98d5-5c4b-b445-1e12f9553b30" :question/math-academy-id "q-342996" :question/problem "Find the real solution to the equation $8\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x=1.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x=\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above definition into the equation $8\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8(\\frac{{e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}) & =1 \\\\ 8({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) & ={e}^{x}+{e}^{-x} \\\\ 8{e}^{x}-8{e}^{-x} & ={e}^{x}+{e}^{-x} \\\\ 7{e}^{x} & =9{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply both sides of the equation by ${e}^{x}$ and solve the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\cdot 7{e}^{x} & ={e}^{x}\\cdot 9{e}^{-x} \\\\ {e}^{2x} & =\\frac{9}{7} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{7}) \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{7}) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{7})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only real solution is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{7}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08959185-7ae8-545f-a27e-c6317b14d655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506692} {:answer/id #uuid "3dd24afb-2e8a-5315-bf56-83e7629e3b50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})$ and $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506822} {:answer/id #uuid "42bb5a15-e42c-5fde-b8fd-dab42d65178f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506834} {:answer/id #uuid "8306f30c-7d39-5a1a-8a33-fa06c26a37c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506969} {:answer/id #uuid "b943a61a-5fba-553b-ba34-318ed7a58bfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42bb5a15-e42c-5fde-b8fd-dab42d65178f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506834} :answer-field/id #uuid "93460c70-4a67-59ae-ac0f-2d86b0582d6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc0cc655-da41-5f5b-b054-719fe2ce042d" :question/math-academy-id "q-9675" :question/problem "Find the real solutions to the equation $5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=5.$" :question/worked-solution "The definitions of $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x$ are\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}),\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above definitions into the equation  $5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}))-\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) & =5 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{x}+\\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-x}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-x} & =5 \\\\ 2{e}^{x}+3{e}^{-x} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply both sides of the equation by ${e}^{x},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\cdot (2{e}^{x}+3{e}^{-x}) & ={e}^{x}\\cdot 5 \\\\ 2{e}^{2x}+3 & =5{e}^{x} \\\\ 2{e}^{2x}-5{e}^{x}+3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in ${e}^{x}$ that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{e}^{x}-3)({e}^{x}-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations:\n\n$$\n2{e}^{x}-3=0,\\,{e}^{x}-1=0\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{x}-3 & =0 &  & \\, & {e}^{x}-1 & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =\\frac{3}{2} &  & \\, & {e}^{x} & =1 \\\\ {e}^{x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2}) &  & \\, & x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ and $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{2})$ are the real solutions to the equation."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507519 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96a96d60-5df7-525f-b9d5-460b27317666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{s-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96a96d60-5df7-525f-b9d5-460b27317666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{s-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507005} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ea34c0d-6f70-52cb-8ac2-9e4d837fa8f2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507313} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b891e4e8-a932-5e13-8640-98de8fa2c1b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-2s-2}{(s-3)(s-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b891e4e8-a932-5e13-8640-98de8fa2c1b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-2s-2}{(s-3)(s-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507085} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1cf17fd-da35-507f-b449-a0c201f4cbdd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1650579-5a44-5386-9786-86764c11f1fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-331915" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=3{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,{X}_{2}(s)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\} &=L\\{3{x}_{1}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\} &=L\\{-2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0) &=3L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0) &=-2L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-4 &=3{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+2 &=-2{X}_{1}(s)+{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$\n\nNow, solving the first equation for ${X}_{1}(s),$ we get\n\n$$\ns{X}_{1}(s)-4=3{X}_{1}(s)\\,⟹\\,{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{4}{s-3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the second equation and solving for ${X}_{2}(s),$ we have\n\n$$\ns{X}_{2}(s)+2=-2(\\frac{4}{s-3})+{X}_{2}(s)\\,⟹\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{-2s-2}{(s-1)(s-3)}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508043 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13ed44a6-241d-5838-9fc9-af9b6f83e61d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507622} {:answer/id #uuid "1578c936-8ad1-508e-aa56-b4990c519451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507624} {:answer/id #uuid "67abf3a2-af43-5891-a4b8-887a1c46a81b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507751} {:answer/id #uuid "7e517eaa-0474-52ae-938e-84dbfaa53de5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507784} {:answer/id #uuid "8dda24db-7965-57a0-84c8-bf571ddd300c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1578c936-8ad1-508e-aa56-b4990c519451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507624} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5d3c470-c959-5590-9ddc-7ea57ee65776" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0cddfc6e-37ce-5b87-9aa0-8b3fda1d2938" :question/math-academy-id "q-90392" :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}},\\,x>1\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{2},$ compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+2X-1].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that $\\text{E}[aX+bY]=a\\text{E}[X]+b\\text{E}[Y]$ for any random variables $X$ and $Y$ and constants $a$ and $b.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+2X-1] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+2\\text{E}[X]-\\text{E}[1] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+2\\text{E}[X]-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the second raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{S}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{3}{x})|}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(3-\\frac{3}{a}) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=3$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}+2X-1] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]+2\\text{E}[X]-1 \\\\  & =3+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}-1 \\\\  & =3+3-1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508063 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d33b258-c127-5e48-89c1-172347927c06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1a/1abbfb9c3aeaaeed74af4948b0c645384d6f20c04c23d435224b855cf1192e6f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507627} {:answer/id #uuid "2e2779e9-a749-57a4-aa3d-9cd1888dce16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1e/1e5ea7ddfefb6f1ef81e75c123c528327618ca73bc01df764d2cf3e9564835e1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507657} {:answer/id #uuid "43aa1de5-a93e-57fb-ba78-684b4247686a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bb/bb5ac2100e6106e62b930a9a0cb94d8ce315dc41c4e15a40c5e02f5c5cacfa82.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507702} {:answer/id #uuid "e23c2923-cf2d-5e5e-9c53-dfb0399ef7c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e7/e7e697fe18c032e596590c68a04f0b01893b44ade18c6fcc732a79bf12ebcf4a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507902} {:answer/id #uuid "fe180236-0e5b-5dc5-967b-2a36380c113d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/54/5430d057eaa68b600ceeecca978212314972653382754767470cca5115c0dca5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43aa1de5-a93e-57fb-ba78-684b4247686a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bb/bb5ac2100e6106e62b930a9a0cb94d8ce315dc41c4e15a40c5e02f5c5cacfa82.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507702} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6d8c285-32aa-5c3d-ae1d-8f1ac9cad4c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e3ff34b-d5cf-5bf6-ba34-eb80d066dc38" :question/math-academy-id "q-27525" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{4}{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/a5/a5b088c08da85c389cb96add374186a898793ec23cf0a3e39270c292fe3d606d.png)\n\nThen, we scale this graph by a factor of $\\frac{1}{4}.$ The $y$-intercept becomes $(0,\\frac{1}{4}),$ and the resulting graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/d5/d563be7169471498c6e0193e3be8493ae6ba119fc3fc7b74b397454b65e6cf57.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508072 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36f81b7b-95bc-5070-8586-1cb395b236dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/84/84edd950272f87c19041041fdb5175dde4bd04ed62b71be16669c76d00d93b38.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507678} {:answer/id #uuid "3f66385e-14cc-547e-ba13-182e0f71ee18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f3/f373cb57189451d27eaed320639d5fdcb8a1fcf6f4b1b2087acc94b6d719e09c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507696} {:answer/id #uuid "89b0b47c-97bc-5ce1-a901-60ba28570aea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/14/1448fe064fcd0ee80b02f6370fdc79be41d3ad655b06738876c5c8ff0c1363bf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507796} {:answer/id #uuid "91c70715-5f59-54ca-ab8d-95c8c925b87f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d6/d620960e531620c0f2fb0ca3d171a6c0d0a6c45348eca914345cd40fd4675245.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507804} {:answer/id #uuid "c3b6b136-4626-540b-bc21-4bbc2d52ae54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f6/f6f93982fecfb664cfcc41a849ce14e5da96c98cc59862cf4a859b5c6e766302.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3b6b136-4626-540