pe {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46a450a1-a396-59e1-9a6b-cb0e767f8fad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507708} :answer-field/id #uuid "55e7eb3d-b4ba-5f6a-8826-1bca21045615" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c063995-cffa-55cf-8462-64cb05fc0c3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-46059" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x}?$\n\nI. It's an increasing function\n\nII. Its domain is $x>0$\n\nIII. $f(x)\\to +\\,\\infty $ as $x\\to +\\,\\infty $" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/45/4531a531fb98f092751cec4552d6ead2bf828759842214f030618dba1ea85893.png)\n\n \n\nNow, let's look at each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true.\n\n- Statement II is false, since the domain of the function $f(x)={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x}$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is true. For $x>0,$ the graph increases without a bound as $x$ increases to infinity.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508084 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "069bd935-a3e1-5a4b-b5b2-e9d5897750e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507609} {:answer/id #uuid "4cb16e5f-ba42-5bd9-90eb-f56bf17bbb1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507715} {:answer/id #uuid "803213b4-62ce-516b-810e-b7062290768e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507790} {:answer/id #uuid "9b850d8c-4056-5cb7-8709-ad0f4ffda5d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507815} {:answer/id #uuid "f6fdd93e-817a-5ad8-aca9-7e39b7a8f224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "069bd935-a3e1-5a4b-b5b2-e9d5897750e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507609} :answer-field/id #uuid "d60905be-5479-5411-b5d1-1ac224418f27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "94e841e5-3216-5691-bf5f-488fa6567b77" :question/math-academy-id "q-47731" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}?$\n\nI. It's an increasing function\n\nII. Its range is $y>\\frac{4}{5}$\n\nIII. $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty $" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/82/8250b7e30d7960f7c06d0e254e8bad97546e959be1ad3128eb491800b6e91533.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\n- Statement II is false. The set of possible outputs of $f(x)={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}$ consists of all the positive numbers, so the range is  $y>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. As $x$ increases, the function value gets smaller and smaller, approaching zero. Therefore $f(x)\\to 0$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508088 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ee41f75-75e8-560d-a0d8-a810a881a517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507693} {:answer/id #uuid "70f163bc-40df-5709-9347-836972090890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507762} {:answer/id #uuid "b4db0f16-b037-5c85-9197-78dadf2c5e6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507846} {:answer/id #uuid "b5ed03b7-54fd-5290-92c9-dacae031c94b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507849} {:answer/id #uuid "e6a08e1c-63f5-575a-8ab3-1f91c228a8cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507910}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5ed03b7-54fd-5290-92c9-dacae031c94b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507849} :answer-field/id #uuid "5aba8ef7-d5cb-5b87-8948-2023f65a58ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9fbf8c06-659f-590c-beea-a6431f0cb137" :question/math-academy-id "q-47746" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}?$\n\nI. Its domain is $x>0$\n\nII. The graph of $y=f(x)$ passes through the point $(-1,4).$\n\nIII. It's an increasing function" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/5d/5d97ca13c1289558c6bb8e8ce50fb1ef5745f54acf27346a32d6451b1bb7f9bb.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}$ is defined for all input values $x,$ so the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is true. When we evaluate the function at $x=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\nf(-1)={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{-1}=4.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508090 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "236bd1ef-36c0-5ab3-a3a7-481538927b09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/92/929a671186aad590fe4f771d9facb822b40fe43b77579720edd38fb8f3d22f25.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507639} {:answer/id #uuid "39a89faa-0990-54b8-a126-7ae1c88fe807" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/35/35ad5f3a89d389e504af26d0f868d81cc876dd676ee26efedb0b545f1aa8329d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507681} {:answer/id #uuid "419f0a6c-2f92-5864-8490-4121d2d37b56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/94/9483b0ff61195c7ed27b17ab75e62b403f085bc84766dd10ae18ce74a77d1c77.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507698} {:answer/id #uuid "4c1c266d-1ea0-5e7b-9c44-dd62b129b698" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4e/4ea17d1fb91660a0b96d8c61baeea86c8e0ae056102121467c008421a093f26b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507714} {:answer/id #uuid "a06a4c81-f923-5ac0-a1dc-7fa6dc3a2c36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a0/a0260da40ebbd07774522c7602b77fe939f312d4fce75c36dd8cc19f39fa12d3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "236bd1ef-36c0-5ab3-a3a7-481538927b09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/92/929a671186aad590fe4f771d9facb822b40fe43b77579720edd38fb8f3d22f25.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507639} :answer-field/id #uuid "e13f8818-a437-5c98-9880-94f58b0fdfb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a5402e2e-2a63-5f18-9868-604f33f81924" :question/math-academy-id "q-29360" :question/problem "Which of the following plots shows the graph of $f(x)={(\\frac{5}{4})}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y={(\\frac{5}{4})}^{x}$ has a $y$-intercept of $(0,1),$ grows rapidly for $x>0,$ and approaches the asymptote $y=0$ for $x<0.$ \n\nWhen $x=1,$ we have $y={(\\frac{5}{4})}^{1}=\\frac{5}{4}.$ Therefore, another point on the graph is $(1,\\frac{5}{4}).$ \n\nWe draw the graph through these points as follows:\n\n![](images/92/929a671186aad590fe4f771d9facb822b40fe43b77579720edd38fb8f3d22f25.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508104 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e10503b-edb6-5b90-9853-cb5f2c3ff972" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1f/1f1caf20957931c2453c07ac84d5a104d6e49951436e88d569e31bbe205b0244.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507630} {:answer/id #uuid "2494f4ae-d21d-5b05-9615-a60fc623cee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bc/bc226e70ed1cd19eca5cca6d82d6e72d4fa1ccd6f243aa7feca9134c11b93ba1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507641} {:answer/id #uuid "2fa46daf-b83a-59a7-a072-244a33736204" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b8/b843d7fa9628622fc74ea9989fddb8b459952369c6bd6b4e9cecd754cb4acf0b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507660} {:answer/id #uuid "d2e41aa6-94d0-5182-b006-2e6cf57099a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/28/286752cfbadff12d52d940b193f37cf4f4fade6da05f239f350905ce128ebabf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507889} {:answer/id #uuid "ff96ccd6-24f2-5ea1-8f70-ebd65b0d2c91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/48/48a219b02ff28ff978dea320428b3afbc6f6cae3b9265b2c21c471ee751e542f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507944}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e10503b-edb6-5b90-9853-cb5f2c3ff972" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1f/1f1caf20957931c2453c07ac84d5a104d6e49951436e88d569e31bbe205b0244.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507630} :answer-field/id #uuid "f06659c0-7b4f-5aa8-8396-f34d621a1627" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d8c01898-d5f3-593c-be7d-e90b6733c0c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-29397" :question/problem "Which of the following plots shows the graph of $f(x)={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}$ has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1),$ grows rapidly for $x<0,$ and approaches the asymptote $y=0$ for $x>0.$\n\nWhen $x=-1,$ we have $y={(\\frac{1}{5})}^{-1}=5.$ Therefore, another point on the graph is $(-1,5).$ \n\nWe draw the graph through these points as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1f1caf20957931c2453c07ac84d5a104d6e49951436e88d569e31bbe205b0244.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508187 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3eb5050b-2f97-532e-b6c8-5866bcc9fa51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3eb5050b-2f97-532e-b6c8-5866bcc9fa51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508188} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac08740b-e844-58dd-baf4-40ef0b885178" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508224} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f39f41a-cfb3-5bf0-b58f-8d17841342c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{17}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f39f41a-cfb3-5bf0-b58f-8d17841342c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{17}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508189} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa771fca-d97d-5b82-add7-3f2815e11284" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3db3cd07-1246-5ea5-a44f-5fee48dd9a13" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x,y)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3x+3y &=2 \\\\ 3x+y &=-5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution is $($ {{field-1}} $, $ {{field-2}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nUsing the second equation, we can isolate $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+y & =-5 \\\\ y & =-5-3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=-5-3x$ into the first equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+3y & =2 \\\\ 3x+3(-5-3x) & =2 \\\\ 3x-15-9x & =2 \\\\ -6x-15 & =2 \\\\ -6x & =17 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{17}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $x=-\\frac{17}{6}$ into $y=-5-3x,$ we can then find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5-3(-\\frac{17}{6}) \\\\ y & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-\\frac{17}{6},\\,y=\\frac{7}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508204 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61123492-b1fa-52ea-a78d-92d15eb4799a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61123492-b1fa-52ea-a78d-92d15eb4799a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508201} :answer-field/id #uuid "00e813eb-39d3-591a-bc56-b776bdfd2316" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508170} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fc36be2-1ad6-5b03-b736-d29211c26e29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fc36be2-1ad6-5b03-b736-d29211c26e29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508194} :answer-field/id #uuid "84b6ed04-ec34-5023-a363-127985a3747a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "76c476e0-406c-5751-a806-f5cbe03fe610" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x,y)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x+4y &=8 \\\\ -2x+y &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution is $($ {{field-1}} $, $ {{field-2}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nUsing the second equation, we can isolate $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+y & =6 \\\\ y & =6+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=6+2x$ into the first equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+4y & =8 \\\\ -3x+4(6+2x) & =8 \\\\ -3x+24+8x & =8 \\\\ 5x+24 & =8 \\\\ 5x & =-16 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{16}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $x=-\\frac{16}{5}$ into $y=6+2x,$ we can then find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =6+2(-\\frac{16}{5}) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-\\frac{16}{5},\\,y=-\\frac{2}{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508227 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e04510d-2fe1-543a-ac55-911f068010c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e04510d-2fe1-543a-ac55-911f068010c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508221} :answer-field/id #uuid "496722ff-acc0-5b76-a13e-09917012d73d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508192} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08a38b64-b8c2-526f-8347-6ba7980eb350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08a38b64-b8c2-526f-8347-6ba7980eb350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508172} :answer-field/id #uuid "b547fc55-eab5-5d24-ba49-2faa2df55e6a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b0966484-f411-5cf8-8677-c24b7843a43c" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x,y)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x-2y &=5 \\\\ 2x+y &=-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution is $($ {{field-1}} $, $ {{field-2}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nUsing the second equation, we can isolate $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+y & =-1 \\\\ y & =-1-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=-1-2x$ into the first equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-2y & =5 \\\\ 5x-2(-1-2x) & =5 \\\\ 5x+2+4x & =5 \\\\ 9x+2 & =5 \\\\ 9x & =3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ into $y=-1-2x,$ we can then find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1-2(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=\\frac{1}{3},\\,y=-\\frac{5}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508233 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "609dae4d-f20b-597d-8d5c-bd3985bfb3ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "609dae4d-f20b-597d-8d5c-bd3985bfb3ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508200} :answer-field/id #uuid "313ebe14-9bc4-580a-8ef0-cc446d43568b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508182} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54ede735-9e0a-5b8d-8cb8-1ec5b24e8a75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54ede735-9e0a-5b8d-8cb8-1ec5b24e8a75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508195} :answer-field/id #uuid "40fbd52d-5989-56ab-8589-60d240620d11" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ddedcc45-5aba-5229-89b3-d8e7abbe4344" :question/problem "Find the solution $(x,y)$ to this system using the method of substitution.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+2y &=12 \\\\ -2x+y &=5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution is $($ {{field-1}} $, $ {{field-2}} $)$" :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nUsing the second equation, we can isolate $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+y & =5 \\\\ y & =5+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=5+2x$ into the first equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & =12 \\\\ 2x+2(5+2x) & =12 \\\\ 2x+10+4x & =12 \\\\ 6x+10 & =12 \\\\ 6x & =2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ into $y=5+2x,$ we can then find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5+2(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ y & =\\frac{17}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=\\frac{1}{3},\\,y=\\frac{17}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508911 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cc1ba37-52fc-50dc-b984-9dc568bfe31c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508478} {:answer/id #uuid "3f0635c7-95c2-5ca0-b103-4151020dda21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508481} {:answer/id #uuid "7affb6d5-dcf4-5d5f-997f-81bf1706c88d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508570} {:answer/id #uuid "fdebe404-8aed-5769-8c60-015f60d99e5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508806} {:answer/id #uuid "fef041c0-b3ba-511a-a1db-5120dc4f98a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508809}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f0635c7-95c2-5ca0-b103-4151020dda21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508481} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e72dc91-9bb2-5a52-9ea8-c8a3392b078f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00868e65-0caa-53d3-bac1-8c3f5e5b6143" :question/math-academy-id "q-118712" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x+2}{x+1})}^{2x+2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x+2}{x+1})}^{2x+2}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x+2}{x+1})}^{2x+2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{(x+1)+1}{x+1})}^{2x+2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{x+1})}^{2(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $x+1=n.$ Therefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x+2}{x+1})}^{2x+2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{x+1})}^{2(x+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{2n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508912 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b41a9298-fc3d-590a-8e9f-31f92e51c962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30+\\frac{60}{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b41a9298-fc3d-590a-8e9f-31f92e51c962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30+\\frac{60}{\\pi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508659} :answer-field/id #uuid "bcf6bf10-deb1-51fd-ba83-03a05512720e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "06dbc133-9492-57ef-8ac9-643d61a66e1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-344853" :question/problem "![](images/9e/9ea7b23d8dd14a0ed092152592800022400ee73e7b371146f5e972900f2c2f29.png)\n\nA community organizer wants to install a border around a walkway made up of a rectangle and two semicircles, as shown in the diagram. She has only $120\\,m$ of border material. The organizer wishes to maximize the area of the **rectangle**. Find $x+2r$ where $x$ and $2r$ are the dimensions of the rectangle that has the greatest area. Do not round the answer.\n\n$x+2r=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let $r$ be the radius of the semicircles (the same for both semicircles) and let $x$ be the length of the other side of the rectangle. We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2rx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter is fixed at $120\\text{ m},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n120=\\pi r+\\pi r+2x=2\\pi r+2x.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $x$ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}120 & =2\\pi r+2x \\\\ 2x & =120-2\\pi r \\\\ x & =60-\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2r(60-\\pi r) \\\\  & =120r-2\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A,$ so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =120-4\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting ${A}^{′}(r)=0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ 120-4\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 4\\pi r & =120 \\\\ r & =\\frac{30}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=\\frac{30}{\\pi }$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(r)=-4\\pi \\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(\\frac{30}{\\pi })=-4\\pi <0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(\\frac{30}{\\pi })$ is negative, we conclude that $r=\\frac{30}{\\pi }$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$\n\nFinally, when $r=\\frac{30}{\\pi }$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =60-\\pi (\\frac{30}{\\pi }) \\\\  & =60-30 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the largest area are $x=30$ and $2r=\\frac{60}{\\pi },$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\nx+2r=30+\\frac{60}{\\pi }=\\frac{30\\pi +60}{\\pi }.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508915 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccade439-4e5d-58b2-8228-d6bfc35a07d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccade439-4e5d-58b2-8228-d6bfc35a07d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508715} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac8dc0c5-5a97-55c6-b567-1d5f02ebaee4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "180b48d4-1e2c-527c-b442-f02b5ba67824" :question/math-academy-id "q-347235" :question/problem "![](images/ab/ab0416aeff61e4916acc1765a7931da0a41a64ba9d0ac4c05a322de6a1ed431b.png)\n\nA box whose depth $z$ is equal to half of its height $y$ has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the top and bottom costs $$4$ per square meter, and the material for the lateral faces costs $$1$ per square meter. The box must have a volume of $36\\,{m}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $z$ for the box that has the smallest production cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}} units" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $z$ such that the manufacturing cost\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =4\\cdot 2xz+1\\cdot 2xy+1\\cdot 2yz \\\\  & =8xz+2xy+2yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume must be $36\\,{m}^{3}.$ So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot z\\cdot y \\\\ 36 & =xz(2z) \\\\ 36 & =2x{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $x$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x{z}^{2} & =36 \\\\ x & =\\frac{18}{{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $C,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =8xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =8\\,(\\frac{18}{{z}^{2}})\\,z+2\\,(\\frac{18}{{z}^{2}})\\,(2z)+2(2z)z \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{z}+\\frac{72}{z}+4{z}^{2} \\\\  & =4{z}^{2}+\\frac{216}{z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${C}^{′}(z)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(z)=8z-\\frac{216}{{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ 8z-\\frac{216}{{z}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 8{z}^{3} & =216 \\\\ {z}^{3} & =27 \\\\ z & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $z=3$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(z)=8+\\frac{432}{{z}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{″}(3)=8+16=24>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${C}^{″}(3)$ is positive, we conclude that $z=3$ is indeed a minimum of $C(z).$\n\nTherefore, the production cost is the smallest when $z=3$ units."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508919 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00940964-97b0-5faa-b185-3728352533b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.76\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508390} {:answer/id #uuid "0bb26296-d74b-52fe-95d8-c6f170dec861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.42\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508404} {:answer/id #uuid "7798b91c-2cf5-5d5c-8c9d-b4d9222d8914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.13\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508561} {:answer/id #uuid "887b771c-1695-5de3-bfd7-ca8cef6a9293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.85\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508593} {:answer/id #uuid "ee27090a-6532-5bde-812c-260b6eef1e2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.73\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7798b91c-2cf5-5d5c-8c9d-b4d9222d8914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.13\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508561} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c84e7ef-df56-5495-af8d-6a63b68ee4c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "35d71232-f38b-500b-9a6f-4af0bdca3a38" :question/math-academy-id "q-18398" :question/problem "![](images/b0/b024db5e134e25212b44cef850118a6823148b01f782c67dccc42f12b82e3854.png)\n\nA piece of cardboard measures $8\\,cm\\times 6\\,cm.$ A tray of height $y\\,\\text{cm}$ is formed by cutting a $y\\times y\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}$ square from each of the corners and folding up the sides, as shown in the diagram. Determine the height of the box that gives the maximum possible volume. Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x,y,$ and $z$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: Notice that the width $x$ and the depth $z$ of the tray are given by\n\n$$\nx=8-2y,\\,z=6-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $x>0$ and $z>0,$ we must have that $8-2y>0$ and $6-2y>0$, hence $y<3.$ Therefore, the range of validity for $y$ is $0<y<3.$\n\n**Step 3**: Substituting the above into the expression for $V,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =xyz \\\\  & =(8-2y)(y)(6-2y) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-28{y}^{2}+48y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=12{y}^{2}-56y+48,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 12{y}^{2}-56y+48 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-14y+12 & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{7\\pm \\sqrt{13}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $\\frac{7+\\sqrt{13}}{3}$ since we already know that $0<y<3.$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $\\frac{7-\\sqrt{13}}{3}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=24y-56.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(\\frac{7-\\sqrt{13}}{3})$ is negative, we conclude that $\\frac{7-\\sqrt{13}}{3}$ is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the stationary point $y=\\frac{7-\\sqrt{13}}{3}≈1.13$ gives the maximum possible volume."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508935 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5a0bdd6-6cc8-57e4-9c9b-50dfb154eeb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5a0bdd6-6cc8-57e4-9c9b-50dfb154eeb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508663} :answer-field/id #uuid "53a722bb-d1a0-58ce-8c66-814e286c6be3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5f708375-cdc1-53f8-8bb4-b17c1643502c" :question/math-academy-id "q-315628" :question/problem "![](images/85/8548c87f15edc7e7c903c753767f12bd6f4c6f0294ff07734659583907f2ea4a.png)\n\nA box whose width $x$ is $2$ times its depth $z$ has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the top and bottom costs $$5$ per square foot, and the material for the lateral faces costs $$2$ per square foot. The box must have a volume of $180\\,{ft}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $z$ for the box that has the smallest production cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}} units" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $z$ such that the manufacturing cost\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =5\\cdot 2xz+2\\cdot 2xy+2\\cdot 2yz \\\\  & =10xz+4xy+4yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume must be $180\\,{ft}^{3}.$ So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot z\\cdot y \\\\ 180 & =2{z}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{z}^{2}y & =180 \\\\ y & =\\frac{90}{{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $C,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =10xz+4xy+4yz \\\\  & =10(2z)z+4(2z)(\\frac{90}{{z}^{2}})+4\\,(\\frac{90}{{z}^{2}})\\,z \\\\  & =20{z}^{2}+\\frac{720}{z}+\\frac{360}{z} \\\\  & =20{z}^{2}+\\frac{1080}{z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${C}^{′}(z)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(z)=40z-\\frac{1080}{{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ 40z-\\frac{1080}{{z}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 40{z}^{3} & =1080 \\\\ {z}^{3} & =27 \\\\ z & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $z=3$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(z)=40+\\frac{2160}{{z}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{″}(3)=40+80=120>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${C}^{″}(3)$ is positive, we conclude that $z=3$ is indeed a minimum of $C(z).$\n\nTherefore, the production cost is the smallest when $z=3$ units."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508936 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "441fd9ba-02ad-5b58-b31f-7123f793b71d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}+\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508489} {:answer/id #uuid "969dac44-be6b-55c3-9e32-bc9b239ac320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508615} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf4b20a-605e-5c2b-bb92-3273af82d50d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x{e}^{3x}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508627} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd05d60-383c-5086-b2f2-a8d58e22437d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x{e}^{3x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508804}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcd05d60-383c-5086-b2f2-a8d58e22437d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x{e}^{3x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508804} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a7bb3c4-5f99-5055-85cf-792009ea367b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508820} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ad355eb-c779-513f-be3e-7ffebc0b039b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508597} {:answer/id #uuid "9a08cb88-e308-5923-8a7a-40f0260d87f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508621} {:answer/id #uuid "a2025edc-5ef5-5831-9d2e-09965e794712" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508634} {:answer/id #uuid "bf951e29-b66d-506c-9922-457610fdd7f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{2}x{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ad355eb-c779-513f-be3e-7ffebc0b039b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508597} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f2b4430-672a-5bf3-807e-80a3453382c0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508857} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "051c9750-97c1-5281-b90e-9994fbef376b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508395} {:answer/id #uuid "7f15f83b-8c0f-5827-b511-b0cca04cf4c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508575} {:answer/id #uuid "a8312813-e54b-52a6-b6b9-51feaefb1de9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508644} {:answer/id #uuid "c8881434-0fe7-5f65-a6ba-6bbdbf42ac37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508703} {:answer/id #uuid "f55f4069-0947-5902-9b9e-4194006e3fe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "051c9750-97c1-5281-b90e-9994fbef376b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508395} :answer-field/id #uuid "a17ac1c5-d149-5ebf-8927-158723ddb605" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fdf7d2e-21a5-5a00-afb9-716691a79836" :question/math-academy-id "q-337475" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{3x})}^{′}-3(u{e}^{3x}) & =6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x}-3u{e}^{3x} & =6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}} & =6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x} & =6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =6{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x=\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =6{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\  & =6{e}^{-3x}(\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{2}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-3x}({e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =3-3{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =3-3{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3-3{e}^{-6x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =3x+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{3x} \\\\  & =3x{e}^{3x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508937 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45e4e7cf-9f38-5d15-8955-648658aadaf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45e4e7cf-9f38-5d15-8955-648658aadaf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508493} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd5596fc-df37-5148-b08d-7e5e925c165e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "64a50494-969c-52da-a2bf-9af25982f51d" :question/math-academy-id "q-346825" :question/problem "![](images/c6/c652b4ce5aa631211824e1f05160bf870c29d3c4d54ea54c29c627aaf7754eea.png)\n\nConsider a box with width $x,$ height $y,$ and depth $z,$ as shown above. The depth of the box is four times its height. Given that the box's volume is $800$ cubic units, what is the smallest possible surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ of the box? Do not round the answer.\n\n${S}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}} square units" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $y$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is $800$ cubic units. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot y\\cdot z \\\\ 800 & =xyz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $z=4y,$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n800=4x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $x$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x{y}^{2} & =800 \\\\ x & =\\frac{200}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =2x(4y)+2xy+2y(4y) \\\\  & =8xy+2xy+8{y}^{2} \\\\  & =10xy+8{y}^{2} \\\\  & =10(\\frac{200}{{y}^{2}})y+8{y}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2000}{y}+8{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(y)=16y-\\frac{2000}{{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 16y-\\frac{2000}{{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 16{y}^{3} & =2000 \\\\ {y}^{3} & =125 \\\\ y & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $y=5$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(y)=16+\\frac{4000}{{y}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(5)=16+32=48>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(5)$ is positive, we conclude that $y=5$ is indeed a minimum.\n\nNow, the smallest surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ that the box can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\text{min}}=S(5) & =8(5)^{2}+\\frac{2000}{5} \\\\  & =600\\,\\text{square units}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508949 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32a56108-53b2-53e0-b3aa-7b816128a40c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508465} {:answer/id #uuid "36174f42-7a31-57ce-a2f7-fc99f9926df2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508470} {:answer/id #uuid "cfac9794-90ef-53f2-8ace-d19fb1866019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508720} {:answer/id #uuid "e4479b0b-ed9e-566c-b900-f0e0cabba3d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508754} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e49bb4-a7ca-56cb-8d47-e747459a6da9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36174f42-7a31-57ce-a2f7-fc99f9926df2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508470} :answer-field/id #uuid "97c59463-67ac-5c37-b808-28ec533a2185" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "87d542d6-aac4-513b-9fc9-107312f9fcdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-61704" :question/problem "Ralf is investigating the effect of a substance on some bacteria. In the beginning, there were $3\\,000$ bacteria. After introducing the substance, Ralf noticed that the number of bacteria reduces by $\\frac{1}{4}$ every minute.\n\nThe function $y(t)$ models the number of bacteria after $t$ minutes and is given by\n\n$$\ny(t)=3\\,000\\cdot {(\\text{___})}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWhat number is missing?" :question/worked-solution "If the number of alive bacteria reduces by $\\frac{1}{4}$ every minute, then $\\frac{3}{4}$ of the bacteria remains every minute. So the decay factor is $\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny=a{b}^{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=3\\,000$ is the initial number of bacteria.\n\n- $b=\\frac{3}{4}$ is the decay factor.\n\n- $t=$ the time, in minutes.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=3\\,000\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{t}.\n$$\n\nSo the missing number is $\\frac{3}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508952 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2ef43e3-fa57-5e96-87ba-acda532bcaf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2ef43e3-fa57-5e96-87ba-acda532bcaf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508635} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e01c9b5-042f-5087-a1d7-da3ece6e125a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "90ab0435-552f-51ca-b675-8d58a2c58837" :question/math-academy-id "q-346764" :question/problem "![](images/1e/1e039fda5af235c4a9948853295846eaadb064fede888b84fdb5be552a35e3f1.png)\n\nConsider a box with width $x,$ height $y,$ and depth $z,$ as shown above. The depth of the box is one-third its width. Given that the box's volume is $288$ cubic units, what is the smallest possible surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ of the box? Do not round the answer.\n\n${S}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}} square units" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $x$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is $288$ cubic units. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot y\\cdot z \\\\ 288 & =xyz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $z=\\frac{x}{3},$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n288=xy(\\frac{x}{3})=\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{3}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{3} & =288 \\\\ {x}^{2}y & =864 \\\\ y & =\\frac{864}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =2x(\\frac{x}{3})+2x(\\frac{864}{{x}^{2}})+2(\\frac{864}{{x}^{2}})(\\frac{x}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1728}{x}+\\frac{576}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{2}+\\frac{2304}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(x)=\\frac{4}{3}x-\\frac{2304}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}x-\\frac{2304}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}x & =\\frac{2304}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3} & =2304 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =1728 \\\\ x & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=12$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(x)=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{4608}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(12)=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}=4>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(12)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=12$ is indeed a minimum.\n\nNow, the smallest surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ that the box can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\text{min}}=S(12) & =\\frac{2}{3}(12)^{2}+\\frac{2304}{12} \\\\  & =96+192 \\\\  & =288\\,\\text{square units}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508954 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3eb3bff0-d429-5165-9782-7f65936a7d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508480} {:answer/id #uuid "690abb71-4d77-510e-a85b-3cd428ff1d32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508548} {:answer/id #uuid "7f70533b-31ac-541b-be61-eba3464f5b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508576} {:answer/id #uuid "8795d4c5-ffa2-5bdb-8d59-fab4fdcfe7bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508592} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b2db0b-70aa-51ed-a1c5-b48983c58ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8795d4c5-ffa2-5bdb-8d59-fab4fdcfe7bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508592} :answer-field/id #uuid "0afcfbf6-047c-53c2-8a23-be6af1196c25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "91c6ce97-3ba3-5af9-8b62-e9a8d179a50b" :question/math-academy-id "q-131070" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{{x}^{2}-1}, & x<1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+1}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nI. $[-5,1)$\n\nII. $[0,5]$\n\nIII. $[5,10]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- The interval $[-5,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now, consider $[0,5].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{{x}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{x(x-1)}{(x-1)(x+1)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x}{x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{1}{x+1})=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[0,5],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, consider $[5,10].$ In this interval $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[5,10].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508958 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2573315e-ce38-5e15-95e7-e11143891fae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$125$ square units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508449} {:answer/id #uuid "2a1eb4e2-b09b-5531-8ce3-4cb94ffd09ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,200$ square units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508454} {:answer/id #uuid "44905299-0cd4-574c-b947-6089c607bb4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$300$ square units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508490} {:answer/id #uuid "6034439d-a48b-58b5-9b55-6d0abcc38a01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$600$ square units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508533} {:answer/id #uuid "75398515-7084-5c1a-8210-e2c60d6cf6e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$480$ square units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6034439d-a48b-58b5-9b55-6d0abcc38a01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$600$ square units" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508533} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd09338f-1eac-5ac9-a363-c02375d91036" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "97252151-ba80-5f2a-b51f-786d26797ca5" :question/math-academy-id "q-117209" :question/problem "![](images/d7/d77c003f88d428578c7f249feb4d0d086c8151c1ef21348d806a0baba751000d.png)\n\nConsider a box with width $x,$ height $y,$ and depth $z,$ as shown above. The width of the box is four times its height. Given that the box's volume is $800$ cubic units, what is the smallest possible surface area of the box?" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $y$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is exactly $800$ cubic units. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot y\\cdot z \\\\ 800 & =xyz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $x=4y,$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n800=4z{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $z$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4z{y}^{2} & =800 \\\\ z & =\\frac{200}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =2(4y)(\\frac{200}{{y}^{2}})+2(4y)y+2y(\\frac{200}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,600}{y}+8{y}^{2}+\\frac{400}{y} \\\\  & =8{y}^{2}+\\frac{2\\,000}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(y)=16y-\\frac{2\\,000}{{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 16y-\\frac{2\\,000}{{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 16{y}^{3}-2\\,000 & =0 \\\\ 16{y}^{3} & =2\\,000 \\\\ {y}^{3} & =125 \\\\ y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $y=5$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(y)=16+\\frac{4\\,000}{{y}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(5)=16+32=48>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(5)$ is positive, we conclude that $y=5$ is indeed a minimum.\n\nNow, the smallest surface area that the box can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(5) & =8(5)^{2}+\\frac{2\\,000}{5}=600.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508959 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "141e356a-c0b9-53fb-a3fa-1c304d6a2600" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508418} {:answer/id #uuid "24156e2e-8841-555a-a19b-1a625b98dde5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508448} {:answer/id #uuid "35e61fc0-40f6-5aef-922f-50f62512b14d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508469} {:answer/id #uuid "5e34251a-f4f3-549b-a7a0-aeaadf3cbdfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508531} {:answer/id #uuid "8cfcba74-2e37-531b-b078-1a79a99a831c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24156e2e-8841-555a-a19b-1a625b98dde5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508448} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac530e0e-d3d1-5a9a-9415-9ac86edc2ff1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "981ba9c5-75b9-5f37-b62a-2b2258f28bdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-118714" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{3x+1}{3x})}^{6x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{3x+1}{3x})}^{6x}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{3x+1}{3x})}^{6x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{3x})}^{2(3x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $3x=n.$ Therefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{3x+1}{3x})}^{6x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{3x})}^{2(3x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{2n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8db0a3b8-eaf2-565b-a867-fb89aa025843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)}sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508605} {:answer/id #uuid "ad7031cc-3266-50a5-b23e-e80324be569a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508652} {:answer/id #uuid "b5605cf5-4e40-5482-98b8-1d348f6bdb6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)}cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508660} {:answer/id #uuid "c1e68085-55c3-57a9-aaa0-14e18af0e330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1e68085-55c3-57a9-aaa0-14e18af0e330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508687} :answer-field/id #uuid "91b8581d-d97f-5540-9faf-641daf95fba0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a25da531-efbb-5c52-a54e-e70d588e0c03" :question/math-academy-id "q-344320" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(1)=6\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $4$-periodic and even. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+5y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny(0)={y}_{c}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,y(1)={y}_{c}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Use $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+B(0)+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).$\n\n- Use $y(1)=6$ with $A=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})+{y}_{p}(1) \\\\ 6 & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})+0 \\\\ B & =\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4e028ec-1409-5643-a03a-1db8045367c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$640$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508637} {:answer/id #uuid "c63d24bd-4157-511a-865f-5dd14362508a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$1\\,280$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508697} {:answer/id #uuid "c99df81f-1e1f-537a-baa9-d9d490cfcc66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$256$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508708} {:answer/id #uuid "d47f863c-e4dd-5cb8-9cc2-dd2c380f778e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$320$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508732} {:answer/id #uuid "ff6283c6-2d8e-5486-863d-299a65917ed1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$1\\,024$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c99df81f-1e1f-537a-baa9-d9d490cfcc66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$256$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508708} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6663f5d-bdaf-51e5-a9ac-ff63ad1ed5e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a3a40784-83f5-5659-8e12-9e2f515c5795" :question/math-academy-id "q-45481" :question/problem "The price of a new bicycle is $$625.$ If the bicycle loses $\\frac{1}{5}$ of its value every year, what will be its value after $4$ years?" :question/worked-solution "Since the value of the bicycle reduces by $\\frac{1}{5}$ per year, $\\frac{4}{5}$ of its value remains every year. \n\nLet's calculate the value of the bicycle after every year:\n\n- After $1$ year, the bicycle will be worth $625\\cdot (\\frac{4}{5})$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, the bicycle will be worth $625\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, the bicycle will be worth $625\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{5})}^{3}$ dollars.\n\n- After $4$ years, the bicycle will be worth $625\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{5})}^{4}$ dollars.\n\nEvaluating the last expression, we get\n\n$$\n625\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{5})}^{4}=256.\n$$\n\nSo after $4$ years, the bicycle will be worth $$256.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508967 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bedd74b-c037-5d37-8e5f-1cba0a6bb7e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There were $75$ reservations per night initially" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508405} {:answer/id #uuid "c0bd5f77-7ec1-5bab-b04b-3f9ff06d4e35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There were $75$ reservations per night each month in $2020$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508685} {:answer/id #uuid "e8038edb-8fa3-5906-a2f7-a4795323716f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The number of reservations per night increased by $75$ each year" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508758} {:answer/id #uuid "f23ec721-40a9-5e3e-97ba-f085f6149342" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The number of reservations per night decreased by $75$ each year" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508782} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e554fa-e8b6-5704-866b-b331bc16cd1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There were $75$ reservations per night $9$ months after March $2020$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bedd74b-c037-5d37-8e5f-1cba0a6bb7e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There were $75$ reservations per night initially" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508405} :answer-field/id #uuid "ade6f4de-4aa6-5980-a9cd-b629e28dce38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a99e73a5-2dae-5b2f-a4fd-53ba3d7abecd" :question/math-academy-id "q-112449" :question/problem "Due to a quarantine initiated in March $2020$, the number of reservations per night in a hotel decreased by one-quarter each month. By the end of $2020,$ the number of reservations per night decreased to $75\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{4})}^{9}.$ What does the factor $75$ mean in this context?" :question/worked-solution "The given expression is an exponential decay expression of the form $y(t)=a\\cdot {b}^{t}$, where we have the following:\n\n- $a=75$ represents the initial number of reservations per night.\n\n- $b=\\frac{3}{4}$ is the decay factor.\n\n- $t=9$ is the time, in months, since March $2020.$\n\nSince $a=75,$ this indicates that there were $75$ reservations initially."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508968 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1de0f2ba-ffe2-5a6b-89cd-556d8f691f99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x+2{e}^{-8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508440} {:answer/id #uuid "572f375a-067a-5a7b-8a7d-bf629da1a48f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x+{e}^{-8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508518} {:answer/id #uuid "e8298673-1ed0-5ba3-a20b-d11c59c9e2e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2x{e}^{-8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508759} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a135e2-6bd6-52bb-b1b1-0b14d3f74e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+x{e}^{-8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "572f375a-067a-5a7b-8a7d-bf629da1a48f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x+{e}^{-8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508518} :answer-field/id #uuid "23bc5b9e-0309-5c0f-a261-a533ceac5bb2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508824} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d1746bc-f878-52ec-b146-aec22500c4a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4x}+x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508406} {:answer/id #uuid "1fb39d55-b678-52fb-8de4-aaf0efa68f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x{e}^{4x}+2{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508443} {:answer/id #uuid "6b1a0ed5-39a5-5b49-889d-036a63baa72f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4x}+2x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508551} {:answer/id #uuid "dc95adb5-f92a-5a3d-adb4-eb25560bfcf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc95adb5-f92a-5a3d-adb4-eb25560bfcf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508741} :answer-field/id #uuid "d34519d1-654b-50eb-a652-7f98090b4427" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508889} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "275b8a15-bc0b-5e5c-86af-632ebcfba8b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{12x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508451} {:answer/id #uuid "a1869588-4b98-59e1-96f4-ff1a600d8834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508632} {:answer/id #uuid "aacafe0e-5f45-594f-af93-bbdab4f15874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508648} {:answer/id #uuid "df1d3c99-484f-5c4a-b945-29f6e6aaa30b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508744} {:answer/id #uuid "e228f210-a90d-5991-975d-f9c2f0b7416a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e228f210-a90d-5991-975d-f9c2f0b7416a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508748} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9324986-dc8e-56cf-affb-81d9274ea958" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "adce0112-6117-576f-afb6-900e0d57f067" :question/math-academy-id "q-337526" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4y=16\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{4x})}^{′}-4(u{e}^{4x}) & =16\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+4u{e}^{4x}-4u{e}^{4x} & =16\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}}-\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}} & =16\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x} & =16\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =16{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x=\\frac{{e}^{4x}-{e}^{-4x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =16{e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡4x \\\\  & =16{e}^{-4x}(\\frac{{e}^{4x}-{e}^{-4x}}{2}) \\\\  & =8{e}^{-4x}({e}^{4x}-{e}^{-4x}) \\\\  & =8-8{e}^{-8x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =8-8{e}^{-8x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 8-8{e}^{-8x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =8x+{e}^{-8x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{4x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{4x} \\\\  & =8x{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508976 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19cd2332-d9e4-5f07-a6f6-b1f461806d71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508431} {:answer/id #uuid "31e0be61-4604-5a58-b042-bc92fc457d48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508463} {:answer/id #uuid "edae2223-31c8-57b8-91a2-c2b71c6e6090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508771} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b08025-f86f-5c45-b65e-e33d0d2c521c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508795} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b94c54-bdfd-5cd4-a505-1c37eeea500c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31e0be61-4604-5a58-b042-bc92fc457d48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508463} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7bcd67e-475b-5366-91b7-b32feba0f989" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c8a37d3e-d129-5ae7-8ba8-cbd709bbd8bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-118721" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2x-1}{2x-2})}^{x-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2x-1}{2x-2})}^{x-1}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2x-1}{2x-2})}^{x-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{(2x-2)+1}{2x-2})}^{x-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{2x-2})}^{x-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{2(x-1)})}^{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $2(x-1)=n$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x-1) & =n \\\\ x-1 & =\\frac{n}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2x-1}{2x-2})}^{x-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{2(x-1)})}^{x-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n/2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508981 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23276101-7865-5bce-a378-809937535d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}\\,in" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508446} {:answer/id #uuid "2ec1e2fe-2b9d-576b-93e5-7123f52b7b55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}\\,in" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508461} {:answer/id #uuid "525d95f6-b301-501d-a8ef-8b62fe4587c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,in" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508511} {:answer/id #uuid "cbd10116-e90b-519e-9bff-6dab94a09c84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,in" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508712} {:answer/id #uuid "ec0d608f-b2b9-5bdb-a629-b7cb6a133bd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}\\,in" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ec1e2fe-2b9d-576b-93e5-7123f52b7b55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}\\,in" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508461} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a47ad8f-644d-5ed1-821f-c9fc750297cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d416390a-8b08-58ae-b4bd-805af1c30ba3" :question/math-academy-id "q-86451" :question/problem "![](images/cf/cf7c07babafa92e2c88e2e08727edc0d724b05f1318f6cf022d9f8b31ff668d7.png)\n\nA piece of cardboard measures $15\\,in\\times 8\\,in.$ A tray of height $y\\,\\text{in}$ is formed by cutting a $y\\times y\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}$ square from each of the corners and folding up the sides, as shown in the diagram. Determine the height of the box that gives the maximum possible volume." :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x,y,$ and $z$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: Notice that the width $x$ and the depth $z$ of the tray are given by\n\n$$\nx=15-2y,\\,z=8-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $x>0$ and $z>0,$ we must have that $15-2y>0$ and $8-2y>0$, hence $y<4.$ Therefore, the range of validity for $y$ is $0<y<4.$\n\n**Step 3**: Substituting the above into the expression for $V,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =xyz \\\\  & =(15-2y)(y)(8-2y) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-46{y}^{2}+120y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=12{y}^{2}-92y+120,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 12{y}^{2}-92y+120 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-23y+30 & =0 \\\\ (3y-5)(y-6) & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{3},\\,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $6$ since we already know that $0<y<4.$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $\\frac{5}{3}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=24y-92.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(\\frac{5}{3})$ is negative, we conclude that $\\frac{5}{3}$ is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the stationary point $y=\\frac{5}{3}$ gives the maximum possible volume."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508982 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f7d7512-00dd-53a1-a339-e816ce6f9bc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.04$ gallons" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508412} {:answer/id #uuid "4a011cf2-f11f-556b-9630-c5475d45f2d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.2$ gallons" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508497} {:answer/id #uuid "9528fa7d-db24-588f-95a3-5f14223601ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ gallon" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508611} {:answer/id #uuid "a758b6c6-f6c3-5428-a865-833457877121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.02$ gallons" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508640} {:answer/id #uuid "d808d943-003d-59e8-a1b2-bb5ce6ae207f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.4$ gallons" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508739}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f7d7512-00dd-53a1-a339-e816ce6f9bc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$0.04$ gallons" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508412} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bbf46a9-a271-5e94-8244-41b134f4a887" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4c0de90-e894-5c05-811c-ac221194e0c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-112129" :question/problem "A five gallons water bottle is leaking its content. The number of gallons of water left inside the bottle after $t$ minutes is given by the formula $W(t)=5\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{t}.$ How many gallons of water will be inside the bottle after $3$ minutes?" :question/worked-solution "To get the number of gallons of water inside the bottle after $3$ minutes, we evaluate the function at $t=3.$ Substituting $t=3$ into the function $W(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(3) & =5\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{3} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{5}^{3}}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{125} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{125} \\\\  & =0.04.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, after $3$ minutes, there will be $0.04$ gallons of water inside the bottle."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508984 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "136d2a61-b0f7-5dd5-8838-447c8e8d76e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{400}{\\pi +2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508416} {:answer/id #uuid "2d1c04e1-50cd-5cdc-bde9-38e71d892e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{200}{\\pi +4}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508456} {:answer/id #uuid "5acae424-da37-50ed-b389-f23ad0386052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508524} {:answer/id #uuid "5e0ef677-0dfb-56b7-b72e-a9eb57ef63cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{100}{\\pi +4}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508530} {:answer/id #uuid "e79c2f39-3294-5d2a-83ce-47805402dd2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{300}{\\pi +2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5acae424-da37-50ed-b389-f23ad0386052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508524} :answer-field/id #uuid "18d6be88-7869-5ea2-874d-dbe10543c41d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d85466ba-cd37-5739-bfc9-c486bbfacc9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-17957" :question/problem "![](images/0d/0da2ed0bca11725e7ffb3fa3c5952807e406530a0625b01ebb476f0145b64e30.png)\n\nA farmer wants to fence a field that is made up of a rectangle and two semicircles, as shown in the diagram. He has only $200\\,m$ of fencing. What is the length $2x$ of the side of the field that covers the greatest area?" :question/worked-solution "First,  let $r$ the radius of the upper semicircle (top) and $x$ the radius of the lateral semicircle (right), hence the dimensions of the rectangle are $2r$ and $2x.$ We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =(2r)(2x)+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi {x}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4rx+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi {x}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter is fixed at $200$ units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n200=\\pi r+\\pi x+2x+2r\\,.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $x$ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}200 & =\\pi r+\\pi x+2x+2r \\\\ x(\\pi +2) & =200-r(\\pi +2) \\\\ x & =\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4rx+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi {x}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4r(\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r)+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}{(\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r)}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A,$ so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =4(\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r)+4r(-1)+\\pi r+\\pi (\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r)(-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{800}{\\pi +2}-4r-4r+\\pi r-\\frac{200\\pi }{\\pi +2}+\\pi r \\\\  & =-8r+2\\pi r+\\frac{800-200\\pi }{\\pi +2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting ${A}^{′}(r)=0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -8r+2\\pi r+\\frac{800-200\\pi }{\\pi +2} & =0 \\\\ 2r(\\pi -4) & =-\\frac{800-200\\pi }{\\pi +2} \\\\ 2r(\\pi -4) & =\\frac{200\\pi -800}{\\pi +2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{100\\pi -400}{(\\pi +2)(\\pi -4)} \\\\ r & =\\frac{100(\\pi -4)}{(\\pi +2)(\\pi -4)} \\\\ r & =\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we confirm that $r=\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(r)=-8+2\\pi \\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(\\frac{100}{\\pi +2})=-8+2\\pi <0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(\\frac{100}{\\pi +2})$ is negative, we conclude that $r=\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$\n\nFinally, when $r=\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-\\frac{100}{\\pi +2} \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the side of the field with the largest area is $2x=\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508985 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18d43fd9-ce54-5930-a946-911c379fee1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{80}{6}}\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508428} {:answer/id #uuid "640087a7-09a2-5c75-8887-63269d193583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{8}{6}}\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508540} {:answer/id #uuid "a90c4b99-8675-5177-b0b8-164a692c771e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{16}{9}}\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508645} {:answer/id #uuid "aa618909-09fa-50e1-9cee-475c9b27fe6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}}\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508647} {:answer/id #uuid "d1fbaa07-68f0-58af-a21d-b167d65507c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{4}{9}}\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa618909-09fa-50e1-9cee-475c9b27fe6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}}\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508647} :answer-field/id #uuid "16e36503-d61e-578e-a1eb-685a25da07ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d9422237-5095-57b7-8cd1-109d7184ed3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-3478" :question/problem "![](images/31/31df5fe79f2aa8715321b6500474683a01732788e5fafc3300dfffc83c266893.png)\n\nA box whose width $x$ is $3$ times its depth $z$ has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the top and bottom costs $$5$ per square foot, and the material for the lateral faces costs $$2$ per square foot. The box must have a volume of $50\\,{ft}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $z$ for the box that has the smallest production cost?" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $z$ such that the manufacturing cost\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =5\\cdot 2xz+2\\cdot 2xy+2\\cdot 2yz \\\\  & =10xz+4xy+4yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume must be $50\\,{ft}^{3}.$ So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot z\\cdot y \\\\ 50 & =3{z}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{2}y & =50 \\\\ y & =\\frac{50}{3{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $C,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =10xz+4xy+4yz \\\\ C & =10(3z)z+4(3z)(\\frac{50}{3{z}^{2}})+4(\\frac{50}{3{z}^{2}})z \\\\ C & =30{z}^{2}+\\frac{200}{z}+\\frac{200}{3z} \\\\ C & =30{z}^{2}+\\frac{800}{3z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${C}^{′}(z)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(z)=60z-\\frac{800}{3{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ 60z-\\frac{800}{3{z}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 180{z}^{3} & =800 \\\\ {z}^{3} & =\\frac{800}{180} \\\\ z & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $z=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(z)=60+\\frac{1\\,600}{{z}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{″}(\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}})>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${C}^{″}(\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}})$ is positive, we conclude that $z=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}}$ is indeed a minimum of $C(z).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508988 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7c8741a-939b-59a0-976b-bf72431b48ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "243" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7c8741a-939b-59a0-976b-bf72431b48ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "243" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508797} :answer-field/id #uuid "eae4d3f6-b44e-59db-a081-02bc4a4fa2c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2adf7b9-c049-5411-a710-9c822b654299" :question/math-academy-id "q-346760" :question/problem "![](images/6f/6f753b0f958204f705f0d59610210ae20e8f3096f0a9847bf72e2d3ab43a6305.png)\n\nConsider a box with width $x,$ height $y,$ and depth $z,$ as shown above. The depth of the box is half its width. Given that the box's volume is $243$ cubic units, what is the smallest possible surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ of the box? Do not round the answer.\n\n${S}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}} square units" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $x$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is $243$ cubic units. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot y\\cdot z \\\\ 243 & =xyz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $z=\\frac{x}{2},$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n243=xy(\\frac{x}{2})=\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{2} & =243 \\\\ {x}^{2}y & =486 \\\\ y & =\\frac{486}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =2x(\\frac{x}{2})+2x(\\frac{486}{{x}^{2}})+2(\\frac{486}{{x}^{2}})(\\frac{x}{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{972}{x}+\\frac{486}{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{1458}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(x)=2x-\\frac{1458}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{1458}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{1458}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ 2{x}^{3} & =1458 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =729 \\\\ x & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=9$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(x)=2+\\frac{2916}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(9)=2+4=6>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(9)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=9$ is indeed a minimum.\n\nNow, the smallest surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ that the box can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\text{min}}=S(9) & ={9}^{2}+\\frac{1458}{9} \\\\  & =243\\,\\text{square units}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508989 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13d5732d-43c0-52d1-8fcd-5b4c29c890e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508417} {:answer/id #uuid "1a77c3bd-e621-5fd7-b6c7-0706a0f09516" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508433} {:answer/id #uuid "4a32cd4b-193c-5d68-8944-db95ecc3226a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508498} {:answer/id #uuid "6a031128-7cbf-5a5c-a079-c9a2ed669acd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508550} {:answer/id #uuid "97e76a18-2641-51b0-a183-4e17294d8733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a32cd4b-193c-5d68-8944-db95ecc3226a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508498} :answer-field/id #uuid "51810a08-334f-5c26-a631-f9e234750786" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e2edca84-13da-5da7-b4b8-ed4b37f13789" :question/math-academy-id "q-26244" :question/problem "A pest controller was responsible for humanely reducing the number of mice within a hotel. If there were $720$ mice initially and $\\frac{5}{6}$ of mice were removed from the hotel every day, how many mice remained in the hotel after two days?" :question/worked-solution "Since the number of mice reduced by $\\frac{5}{6}$ per day, then only $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the mice from the previous day remained.\n\nSo we have:\n\n- At the start, there were $720$ mice.\n\n- After $1$ day, there were $720\\cdot (\\frac{1}{6})$ mice.\n\n- After $2$ days, there were $720\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}$ mice.\n\nEvaluating the last expression, we get\n\n$$\n720\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}=20.\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $2$ days only $20$ mice remained."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508995 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06093a48-e545-54b9-8ae5-487035b494fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.691" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06093a48-e545-54b9-8ae5-487035b494fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.691" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508397} :answer-field/id #uuid "82753b1d-4114-53cd-9ba7-e76ac29f8bca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508851}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "efd53794-367f-547c-a3cb-0e0a7df2cfac" :question/math-academy-id "q-276035" :question/problem "If $X∼B(4,\\frac{1}{3}),$  then, **rounded to $3$ decimal places**, $P(1\\le X<3)≈${{field-1}} ." :question/worked-solution "For a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ where $n$ is the number of trials, and $p$ is the probability of success on each trial, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $X∼B(4,\\frac{1}{3}),$ so $X$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4-x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1\\le X<3) & =P(X\\in \\{1,2\\}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}){(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4-2} \\\\  & =4{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}+6{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈0.691\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508998 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "042154b1-fd7b-5ea0-a9dd-903c37c5121d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,in" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508394} {:answer/id #uuid "6961a8a1-a684-5ee8-a675-a2847e5fc708" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,in" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508549} {:answer/id #uuid "954a97db-91e2-5d6b-ab5d-4b9819568a15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\,in" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508612} {:answer/id #uuid "9b149e48-1ae1-5688-a860-750568060011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}\\,in" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508622} {:answer/id #uuid "c98ae24e-d838-52a1-a11a-fea3b0533b58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}\\,in" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c98ae24e-d838-52a1-a11a-fea3b0533b58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}\\,in" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508707} :answer-field/id #uuid "d64e9272-ea26-5829-b8f3-83104e2c4323" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fcc5cb30-3a54-5c1c-aecb-10ee16fa7222" :question/math-academy-id "q-117300" :question/problem "![](images/dd/dd5731dcea0cc6bc8ffc0aad963f4b05b57a7422512a2df3df64666e63254f77.png)\n\nA piece of cardboard measures $10\\,in\\times 10\\,in.$ A tray of height $y\\,\\text{in}$ is formed by cutting a $y\\times y\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}$ square from each of the corners and folding up the sides, as shown in the diagram. Determine the height of the box that gives the maximum possible volume." :question/worked-solution "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV={x}^{2}y\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: Notice that the side length $x$ of the tray is given by\n\n$$\nx=10-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $x>0,$ we must have that $10-2y>0$ hence $y<5.$ Therefore, the range of validity for $y$ is $0<y<5.$\n\n**Step 3**: Substituting the above into the expression for $V,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={x}^{2}y \\\\  & =(10-2y)^{2}y \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-40{y}^{2}+100y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=12{y}^{2}-80y+100,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 12{y}^{2}-80y+100 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-20y+25 & =0 \\\\ (3y-5)(y-5) & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{3},\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $5$ since we already know that $0<y<5.$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $\\frac{5}{3}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=24y-80.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(\\frac{5}{3})=-40$ is negative, we conclude that $\\frac{5}{3}$ is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the stationary point $y=\\frac{5}{3}$ gives the maximum possible volume."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509624 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6006dff6-96a2-5d0e-84a0-3b03e4ed0604" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-8,-7⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509583} {:answer/id #uuid "bfc03e5e-4ea9-52d8-9a61-6b9247c4e1a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-4,7⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509609} {:answer/id #uuid "e00acdb9-77e7-5763-b728-9a46dcbc6a7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨8,- \\frac{7}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509615} {:answer/id #uuid "ee86038d-2ddd-5977-8726-a49c3aaba9d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨8,7⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509621} {:answer/id #uuid "fd1340d8-b6f5-5d34-98e8-55a647460747" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-6,- \\frac{11}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee86038d-2ddd-5977-8726-a49c3aaba9d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨8,7⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509621} :answer-field/id #uuid "89d74635-1ee1-55da-9dd0-fb4911dce693" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f61be2ed-92ee-5010-a0f8-babf1a0e8712" :question/problem "If $\\overset{\\to }{A}=⟨-4,- \\frac{7}{2}⟩$, then $-2\\overset{\\to }{A}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute $-2\\overset{\\to }{A},$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\overset{\\to }{A} & =-2\\cdot ⟨-4,- \\frac{7}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(-2)\\cdot (-4),(-2)\\cdot (- \\frac{7}{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "991358c9-3100-5464-96bd-f2c50332af98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510008} {:answer/id #uuid "9d04ddc7-88d1-5961-b149-0ed80dedcd03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510016} {:answer/id #uuid "c2c797d4-4e44-5a31-83c8-b4dceca7a07d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510098} {:answer/id #uuid "cbd3705e-81e5-5864-b1ab-6314a9b9569b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510117} {:answer/id #uuid "f86da36a-fea9-5185-a415-54e83a58db6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "991358c9-3100-5464-96bd-f2c50332af98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510008} :answer-field/id #uuid "31551476-3b52-57ca-b4de-8bda2f7a95d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1ccedda5-a7d6-563d-a624-831df95d1f38" :question/math-academy-id "q-96039" :question/problem "$$\n\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]}^{A}=\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{aligned}]}^{Q}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{aligned}]}^{R}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained from the columns of $A$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, find  the value of ${q}_{11}\\cdot {r}_{12}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1}\\}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{24}{12}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & 4\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]}^{A}=\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}^{Q}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & 4\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{12}\\cdot {r}_{22}=(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})\\cdot (4\\sqrt{3})=-4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510372 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3946dc56-0bee-583a-bd40-5a608326600a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "periodic" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509810} {:answer/id #uuid "8146f9e6-f191-536d-9fc2-a56