db/ref 17592186509755} {:answer/id #uuid "2da9d709-0215-57a2-8672-e6206c6adafe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509784} {:answer/id #uuid "7f765ddc-fd83-5d86-95d4-4600a92c5521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3n\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509967} {:answer/id #uuid "be5da26b-6371-53a4-9ac5-17f64b40bfc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510090} {:answer/id #uuid "c7412e64-4513-5280-aa0d-2cc6735ca7f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3n\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510106} {:answer/id #uuid "d5c4c7a7-3555-51c9-990c-07a246dd3fc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be5da26b-6371-53a4-9ac5-17f64b40bfc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510090} :answer-field/id #uuid "d46cd0be-3da6-58f1-a00a-a7431aa0897f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510344} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2832c392-61c0-50a2-8169-320867050a00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509771} {:answer/id #uuid "2eb52a18-f4ee-5b25-978c-7f06c34831dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2832c392-61c0-50a2-8169-320867050a00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509771} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9a5ddff-f037-5246-a150-e944038eee10" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510348} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02d02289-48c0-58d1-be12-9e4164bd26d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{72(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509701} {:answer/id #uuid "9be14ef7-ca17-5724-aae1-6e23ad9abc2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510013} {:answer/id #uuid "ce7ebafd-9c9a-5118-9114-ac71ef6b796a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{72(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510123} {:answer/id #uuid "e647d9d2-d801-509a-ac89-f72e19a7ccba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e647d9d2-d801-509a-ac89-f72e19a7ccba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510176} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6e028ab-a36c-5469-ba63-ab22fef00ac7" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d451e41-c662-5263-b3f7-8483ecaa8976" :question/math-academy-id "q-342964" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-2{x}^{2}+4$ defined on $[-3,3).$  Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension of $G(x)=-4x$ from the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n-2{x}^{2}+4∼-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{72(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}}\nand\n{{field-3}}\non $R,$ we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n-4x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-4}}\\cdot {{field-5}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and we are already given the coefficients ${a}_{0}$ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{72(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-3,3),$\n\n- continuous on $[-3,3),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-3)=F(3),$ if we extend the value at $x=3$ using the formula $F(x)=-2{x}^{2}+4.$\n\nTherefore, the $6$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{continuous}$ and $\\text{piecewise smooth}$ on $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{x}^{2}+4) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{72(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ -4x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{72(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ -4x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-24(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\ -4x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{24(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510401 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09c1d491-402e-555e-a666-7f7c4e87d6ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4.225-0.35\\theta -2{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509710} {:answer/id #uuid "78a7b057-e13c-59a5-9c17-b9e7479f7400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.45+0.7\\theta +4{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509944} {:answer/id #uuid "811cdd6d-776a-59e7-8f58-71af09a1c53a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4.225-0.7\\theta -2{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509970} {:answer/id #uuid "8f42f131-75fe-5641-ab8b-d247827641d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.225+0.35\\theta +2{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509996} {:answer/id #uuid "ff1fb752-a169-5228-a97e-05992483d175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8.45-0.7\\theta -4{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "811cdd6d-776a-59e7-8f58-71af09a1c53a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4.225-0.7\\theta -2{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509970} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f8fd110-202a-5667-bf26-f7d318d06cc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "67f89f72-de60-5983-9ba7-f68316fa9c35" :question/math-academy-id "q-159972" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,1),$  where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean.  Given that the likelihood function of the sample $0.3,\\,1.2,\\,-2.6,\\,0.4$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=\\frac{1}{4{\\pi }^{2}}\\,{e}^{\\,g(\\theta )},\n$$\n\nwhat is the expression for $g(\\theta )?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{x-\\theta }{\\sigma })}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}.$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =0.3+1.2+(-2.6)+0.4 \\\\  & =-0.7 \\\\ C & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(0.3)^{2}+(1.2)^{2}+(-2.6)^{2}+(0.4)^{2} \\\\  & =8.45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=4.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-4/2}\\,{e}^{-(8.45-2(-0.7)\\theta +4{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4{\\pi }^{2}}\\,{e}^{\\,-4.225\\,-\\,0.7\\theta \\,-\\,2{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(\\theta )=-4.225-0.7\\theta -2{\\theta }^{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510406 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c7dbb34-59dd-5e81-a863-a5c9d70d0ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510014} {:answer/id #uuid "a417d5b7-1f44-5776-946d-4b89b7864438" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510030} {:answer/id #uuid "a7c2e494-cb43-5ca0-8464-d42be445ccc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510042} {:answer/id #uuid "c5953b51-f0b1-5657-955a-863eedbda8b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510102} {:answer/id #uuid "c59ab5af-5a91-5ef6-bd69-64c38848927c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c59ab5af-5a91-5ef6-bd69-64c38848927c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510103} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9d445ab-f7a7-5bd0-ba53-a5e0545e4523" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f844afb-5020-5b9d-a059-ebc3dcd000de" :question/math-academy-id "q-117360" :question/problem "![](images/08/08f6351b8b95c87386d07466b6e876d72f33bc00034e83b360d03d23633d363d.png)\n\nWe need to create a cylindrical tin with a lid using $6\\pi \\,{ft}^{2}$ of sheet metal. What is the radius $r$ of the cylinder that has the largest volume?" :question/worked-solution "First, let $r$ be the radius and $h$ be the height of the cylinder.\n\nWe wish to maximize the volume\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nWe know that the surface area is fixed at $6\\pi \\,{cm}^{2},$ so we also have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n2\\pi rh+2\\pi {r}^{2}=6\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi rh & =6\\pi -2\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ h & =\\frac{6\\pi -2\\pi {r}^{2}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ h & =\\frac{3-{r}^{2}}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $V,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(r) & =\\pi {r}^{2}(\\frac{3-{r}^{2}}{r}) \\\\ V(r) & =(3r-{r}^{3})\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $V,$ so we need to differentiate $V$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(r)=(3-3{r}^{2})\\pi .\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ (3-3{r}^{2})\\pi  & =0 \\\\ 3 & =3{r}^{2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =1 \\\\ r & =1\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that, in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the radius $r$ must be positive.\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=1$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative ${V}^{″}(r)$ is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(r)=-6\\pi r,\n$$\n\nand we can see that ${V}^{″}(1)<0.$ So, the stationary point $r=1$ is indeed a maximum of $V(r).$\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the largest volume has a radius of $r=1\\,\\text{ft}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0abcfa87-c2d8-55b9-8b6c-f6e6dc2c2e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509713} {:answer/id #uuid "185a6029-68c4-5828-8b11-ace521807408" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509734} {:answer/id #uuid "986be587-2ecf-570e-a688-8d2405f238d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510006} {:answer/id #uuid "dadc21a4-2efd-53e9-80c6-696e78b43e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510154} {:answer/id #uuid "fd82aff3-659c-5501-969a-3e3bd0a5021f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "986be587-2ecf-570e-a688-8d2405f238d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{ft}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510006} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ef94707-58e0-523c-a81d-81716ae5304a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "904e690d-cf3b-5b36-80f3-f72dec8825a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-86220" :question/problem "A manufacturer wishes to build a cylindrical container with a base but no lid. The container must have an exact volume of $72\\pi \\,{ft}^{3}.$\nTo make the container, the manufacturer uses two materials:\n\n- a plastic sheet that costs $$1.5$ per ${ft}^{2}$ for the lateral surface,\n\n- a plastic sheet that costs $$0.5$ per ${ft}^{2}$ for the base.\n\nWhat is the radius of the container that gives the smallest manufacturing cost?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/95/959050ee306a96db3745f84bc7095d4e83a23ceadd819f0b749c04b20d5778d2.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the manufacturing cost. We can express the manufacturing cost $C$ in terms of $r$ and $h$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =2\\pi rh\\cdot 1.5+\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =3\\pi rh+0.5\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $72\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n72\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constant equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{72}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(r) & =3\\pi r(\\frac{72}{{r}^{2}})+0.5\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{216\\pi }{r}+0.5\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $C,$ so we need to differentiate $C$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{216\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+\\pi r.\n$$\n\n \n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{216\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+\\pi r & =0 \\\\ \\pi r & =\\frac{216\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ \\pi {r}^{3} & =216\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =216 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{216} \\\\ r & =6\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<6$ then ${C}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>6$ then ${C}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=6$ is indeed a minimum of $C(r).$\n\nTherefore, the container with the smallest manufacturing cost has radius $r=6\\,\\text{ft}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510427 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b4a6c19-602c-54ee-8152-81351ed0e7e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186509741} {:answer/id #uuid "c4704d4c-6962-5e51-9c39-966e80ebd0ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510100} {:answer/id #uuid "d1cdea9b-fce1-5271-9532-93edde53a638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "26\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510134} {:answer/id #uuid "d7cff4ee-3f14-55d3-aab1-becaa9d5c30e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510146} {:answer/id #uuid "f62f9599-77a5-5414-840d-7de4098038df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f62f9599-77a5-5414-840d-7de4098038df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510214} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7e8ebb0-e3d6-56f0-bb35-2dd8e698b8c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c0fb2ee-81ac-5d75-ab41-5ee64f815cef" :question/math-academy-id "q-117348" :question/problem "We wish to construct a cylindrical can with a base and a lid. Our cylinder must have an exact volume of $2\\,000\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ What is the radius of the cylinder that gives the smallest surface area?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/33/33c90b487d2b3036068acf4b771d2ca6ae95e8e8446b706b85fed058dc12a0bf.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi rh+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $2\\,000\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n2\\,000\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{2\\,000}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(r) & =2\\pi r(\\frac{2\\,000}{{r}^{2}})+2\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\,000\\pi }{r}+2\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $S,$ so we need to differentiate $S$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{4\\,000\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{4\\,000\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+4\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 4\\pi r & =\\frac{4\\,000\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{3} & =4\\,000\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =1\\,000 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{1\\,000} \\\\ r & =10\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<10$ then ${S}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>10$ then ${S}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=10$ is indeed a minimum of $S(r).$\n\nTherefore, the cylinder with the smallest surface area has radius $r=10\\,\\text{cm}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510441 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4e759ae-9980-5d3f-9810-8593734c7be3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4e759ae-9980-5d3f-9810-8593734c7be3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510076} :answer-field/id #uuid "329ea85a-eb09-5964-bdf1-0a1e6d289646" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac9bf5e8-0b9b-5a4e-90d8-e2b158eca7f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-345153" :question/problem "We wish to construct a cylindrical can with a base but no lid. Our cylinder must have an exact volume of $8\\pi \\,{in}^{3}.$ What is the smallest possible surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ of the cylinder? Do not round the answer.\n\n${S}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{in}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw a picture of a cylinder. Let $r$ be the radius, and $h$ the height.\n\n![](images/7f/7fe5dad64f7c065e693ed407e0f291ec995b4823a0356e11055d20a0ca89a53b.png)\n\nWe wish to minimize the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi rh+\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the volume is fixed at $8\\pi \\,{in}^{3},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n8\\pi =\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $h$ the subject of the constraint equation, we have that\n\n$$\nh=\\frac{8}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $S,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(r) & =2\\pi r(\\frac{8}{{r}^{2}})+\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{r}+\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $S,$ so we need to differentiate $S$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(r)=-\\frac{16\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+2\\pi r.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives a stationary point, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{16\\pi }{{r}^{2}}+2\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 2\\pi r & =\\frac{16\\pi }{{r}^{2}} \\\\ 2\\pi {r}^{3} & =16\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{3} & =8 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\ r & =2\\,\\text{in}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator, we can verify that if $r<2$ then ${S}^{′}(r)<0,$ and if $r>2$ then ${S}^{′}(r)>0,$ which means that the stationary point $r=2$ is indeed a minimum of $S(r).$\n\nFinally, to find the smallest surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}},$ we substitute $r=2\\,\\text{in}$ into the equation for the surface area $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\text{min}}=S(2) & =\\frac{16\\pi }{2}+\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\  & =8\\pi +4\\pi  \\\\  & =12\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
